export const UNITS = [{"id":"u1","title":"NSPIRE Foundations","chapters":["ch1","ch2","ch3"]},{"id":"u2","title":"Scoring & Deficiencies","chapters":["ch4","ch5","ch6"]},{"id":"u3","title":"Programs, Appeals & Business","chapters":["ch7","ch8"]}];
export const CHAPTERS = [{"id":"ch1","unit":"u1","number":1,"title":"What Is NSPIRE?","subtitle":"The foundation — what replaced UPCS and HQS, what is actually in force today, and where your pre-inspection business lives","sections":[{"heading":"The standard, the agency, and the words that matter","body":"NSPIRE stands for National Standards for the Physical Inspection of Real Estate. It is HUD's single physical-condition standard for assisted housing, and it replaced two older systems that had run in parallel for decades:\n\n• UPCS (Uniform Physical Condition Standards) — the scored, sampled inspection used for Public Housing and project-based Multifamily properties, administered by REAC.\n• HQS (Housing Quality Standards) — the pass/fail inspection used for Housing Choice Vouchers and other tenant-based programs, administered by individual PHAs.\n\nNSPIRE merges the criteria. One list of standards now describes what a defect is, no matter which program pays the subsidy. What still differs by program is what HUD does with the result: some programs get a 0-100 score, others get a pass/fail decision. Chapter 7 covers that split in full.\n\nThe three words you will use constantly, and which people routinely misuse:\n\nSTANDARD. A named inspectable item with a written definition and one or more defined deficiencies. \"Guardrail\" is a standard. \"Mold-like Substance\" is a standard. HUD publishes each one as its own document with its own criteria and correction timeframes.\n\nDEFICIENCY. A specific observed condition that meets the written criteria under a standard. Not \"the railing looks bad\" — a deficiency is \"a guardrail is required at this location and none is present,\" which is a defined bullet inside the Guardrail standard.\n\nSEVERITY. The tier HUD has assigned to that specific deficiency: Life-Threatening, Severe, Moderate, or Low. Severity is a property of the deficiency as written in the standard. It is not the inspector's opinion, and it is not something you argue about on site. Chapter 3 covers all four.\n\nThe single most important habit to build now: when you are in a unit and you see something wrong, do not ask \"how bad is this?\" Ask \"which standard does this fall under, and which of that standard's listed deficiencies does it match?\" The severity comes with the answer. Consultants who guess at severity lose credibility the first time a client checks their work against the actual report.\n\nWhy the vocabulary is worth this much attention: your entire value proposition is that you can predict what an inspector will write down. An inspector writes down standards and deficiencies. If you speak in adjectives, you cannot predict anything."},{"heading":"Why HUD built it — and what actually changed","body":"HUD's stated reasons for NSPIRE, and what each one means in practice:\n\n1. Shift the focus to the inside of the unit, where residents live. Under UPCS, a property could score well on curb appeal while units were in poor shape. NSPIRE weights the Unit inspectable area most heavily and adds a separate Unit Threshold that fails a property on in-unit conditions alone, even when the overall score would otherwise pass. This is the single biggest structural change and it is covered in Chapters 4 and 14.\n\n2. Prioritize health and safety over cosmetics. Deficiencies are graded by the harm they can cause a resident, not by how visible or expensive they are. A scuffed wall in a hallway and a missing outlet cover are not close in severity, and NSPIRE makes the gap explicit.\n\n3. Make criteria measurable rather than judgment-based. This is what you should care about most as a consultant. UPCS had a lot of \"unsatisfactory condition\" language. NSPIRE gives you numbers: a trip hazard is a 3/4-inch vertical differential, not \"uneven.\" A sprinkler head is obstructed at 18 inches, not \"too close to storage.\" Mold-like substance changes severity at 4 square inches, 1 square foot, and 9 square feet. You can carry a tape measure and be right.\n\n4. Add affirmative requirements — things that must be PRESENT, not just things that must not be broken. Under HQS, an inspector largely looked for defects. NSPIRE adds requirements like a minimum number of working outlets in each habitable room, a permanently installed light fixture in kitchens and bathrooms, and GFCI protection near water. A unit with nothing broken can still fail because something required was never installed. This category has its own live compliance date and is covered later in this chapter and in the Regulatory Updates chapter.\n\n5. Unify inspection data. One standard means HUD can compare conditions across Public Housing, Multifamily, vouchers, and CPD programs on the same terms.\n\nWhat did NOT change: the passing score is still 60, the score is still 0-100, REAC still runs the scored inspections, and a sample of units is still inspected rather than every unit."},{"heading":"What is in force right now — the compliance calendar","body":"NSPIRE has been implemented in stages, and HUD has extended several of those stages more than once. Getting this wrong in front of a client is expensive, so learn the current picture and re-check it before every engagement.\n\nPUBLIC HOUSING and PROJECT-BASED MULTIFAMILY (PBRA)\nNSPIRE standards took effect October 1, 2023. These portfolios are inspected under NSPIRE today and receive a 0-100 score. This is the mature part of the program.\n\nTHE AFFIRMATIVE REQUIREMENTS — scoring begins October 1, 2026\nHUD issued joint Notice PIH 2025-27 / H 2025-05 on September 30, 2025, postponing the SCORING of six affirmative requirements to October 1, 2026:\n• Fire-Labeled Doors\n• Electrical — GFCI or AFCI outlet or breaker\n• Guardrail\n• HVAC\n• Interior Lighting\n• Minimum Electrical and Lighting\n\nRead that carefully, because it is the most misunderstood fact in the program right now. These items are still inspected. They are still cited on the report. They still require correction. They simply do not deduct points until October 1, 2026. A property with GFCI missing throughout can be carrying a large latent deduction that arrives all at once on that date.\n\nHOUSING CHOICE VOUCHER, PROJECT-BASED VOUCHER, and SECTION 8 MOD REHAB\nCompliance has been extended to February 1, 2027. This is the third extension of this date. PIH Notice 2026-18, issued July 15, 2026, carried the current guidance and also announced that HUD decommissioned the NSPIRE-V demonstration application and paused development of its own inspection software — PHAs are expected to use their own systems. Until a PHA transitions, its voucher inspections may still run under HQS.\n\nCPD PROGRAMS (ESG, Continuum of Care, HOME, Housing Trust Fund)\nCompliance extended to October 1, 2026.\n\nALWAYS IN EFFECT, NO EXTENSION\nSmoke alarm and carbon monoxide alarm requirements are statutory. They were never part of any extension and apply regardless of program or transition status.\n\nThe consultant's habit: at the start of every engagement, confirm (a) which program the property is in, (b) whether that program's NSPIRE date has arrived, and (c) whether the affirmative-requirement scoring date has passed. Three questions, ninety seconds, and it prevents the two most common ways of being wrong about a property."},{"heading":"The three kinds of NSPIRE inspection you will encounter","body":"SCORED REAC INSPECTIONS (Public Housing and PBRA Multifamily)\nAn inspector contracted by or working for REAC inspects a statistical sample of units plus the Inside and Outside areas. The property receives a 0-100 score. A score of 60 or above passes; below 60 fails. A separate Unit Threshold can force a fail on in-unit conditions alone. The score sets the next inspection interval, feeds HUD asset-management decisions, and is public. This is the inspection your pre-inspection service is designed to simulate.\n\nPASS/FAIL PROGRAM INSPECTIONS (HCV, PBV, and CPD programs)\nA PHA or grantee inspector inspects the individual unit. There is no score. The unit passes or fails, and failed items must be corrected within the timeframe attached to their severity, or the assistance payment is affected. The same standards apply; the consequence is different.\n\nANNUAL SELF-INSPECTIONS\nSeparate from anything HUD sends an inspector to do, PHAs and owners under an assistance contract must self-inspect their properties annually, including all units, against the NSPIRE standards. The results must be retained and produced to HUD on request. This requirement is easy to skip and easy to prove you skipped — and it is one of the cleanest recurring-revenue services a consultant can sell, because it is required every year, covers every unit rather than a sample, and creates the documentation trail that supports a technical review later.\n\nA fourth thing you should know exists, though it is not an inspection type: the DRAFT report. For scored inspections, REAC issues a draft report with a draft score and the full defect record, and then a FINAL report with a final score after the technical review window closes. The gap between those two documents is where the appeals work in Chapter 8 happens."},{"heading":"Where your business actually lives","body":"Strip away the marketing and there are exactly four things a client will pay you for. Know which one you are selling in any given conversation.\n\n1. PREDICTION. \"What will we score if REAC walks in on Tuesday?\" You inspect against the same standards, apply the same Defect Impact Weights, and produce a projected score with a confidence range. This is the service that gets you in the door, because it converts an unbounded fear into a number.\n\n2. PRIORITIZATION. \"We have $40,000 and 200 line items. What do we fix?\" This is where you earn the most and where most competitors are weakest. Because the scoring formula divides every deduction by the number of units in the sample, and because severity tiers are far apart, the highest-scoring repair is frequently not the most expensive or the most visible one. A consultant who can rank a punch list by points-recovered-per-dollar is doing something the maintenance supervisor genuinely cannot do.\n\n3. REMEDIATION MANAGEMENT. Running the corrections: sequencing, vendor coordination, verifying the fix meets the written criterion rather than merely looking better, and documenting it.\n\n4. DOCUMENTATION AND APPEALS. Building the evidence file during the work so that a technical review request has photographs, dates, invoices, and certificates already assembled. Chapter 8 covers this.\n\nThe structural reason the opportunity exists: NSPIRE changed the rules for the entire assisted-housing stock at once, the rules keep moving (three extensions on the voucher date alone), and six major requirement categories switch from unscored to scored on October 1, 2026. Property managers are busy operating buildings. Very few of them have read the standards documents. That gap is the business.\n\nA closing caution that will save you a client someday: your projection is a projection. You are not REAC, you do not control which units get sampled, and an inspector can find something you did not. Say so out loud, in writing, every time. Consultants who promise a specific score are one bad sample away from losing the account. Consultants who promise a defensible methodology and a documented punch list keep the account for years."}]},{"id":"ch2","unit":"u1","number":2,"title":"The 3 Inspectable Areas","subtitle":"Unit, Inside, Outside — the boundary rules, the edge cases that trip people up, and why the same defect is worth different points in different places","sections":[{"heading":"Why the area matters before the severity does","body":"Every NSPIRE deficiency carries two coordinates: WHERE it was found and HOW BAD it is. The scoring formula needs both. Get the area wrong and your projected score is wrong even if your severity call was perfect.\n\nThe three areas:\n\nUNIT — the interior components of an individual dwelling, where the resident lives.\nINSIDE — common areas and building systems inside the building envelope that are not inside a dwelling unit: corridors, stairwells, lobbies, community rooms, laundry rooms, mechanical and boiler rooms, offices, elevators.\nOUTSIDE — the site and the exterior of the building: the building envelope (roof, exterior walls, foundation, exterior doors and windows), attached porches, patios, balconies, carports, fire escapes, plus grounds, walkways, parking, roads, storm drainage, site lighting, fencing, retaining walls, mailboxes, project signs, refuse disposal, play areas and equipment, detached garages and non-dwelling buildings.\n\nThe point weighting runs Unit > Inside > Outside for every severity level. The precise numbers are in Chapter 14, but the ordering is what you need to internalize now: the identical defect costs the most in a unit and the least outside. HUD did this deliberately — the entire design intent of NSPIRE was to move attention to where residents actually live.\n\nThere is a second reason area matters, and it is the one that fails properties: the Unit Threshold. Deductions arising from the Unit area are tested separately against a 30-point limit. Cross that limit and the property fails even when the overall score would have passed. Nothing similar exists for Inside or Outside. So a misfiled deficiency does not just move a fraction of a point — it can move a property across a pass/fail line. Chapter 4 covers the mechanics.\n\nPractical rule for your field notes: record the area on the same line as the deficiency, always, at the moment you observe it. Reconstructing area assignments from photographs three days later is how consultants produce projections that do not match the real report."},{"heading":"Area 1 — Unit: what counts and where the boundary sits","body":"The Unit area covers the interior of an individual dwelling: its rooms, surfaces, fixtures, appliances, plumbing, electrical devices, heating and cooling equipment serving that unit, doors and windows as experienced from inside, and the safety devices installed within it.\n\nWhat you will spend most of your Unit time on:\n• Electrical — outlets, switches, cover plates, exposed conductors, GFCI protection near water, panels and breakers serving the unit, permanent light fixtures.\n• Plumbing — supply and drain leaks, fixtures that do not function, water heater and its temperature and pressure relief assembly when located in the unit, hot and cold running water.\n• Kitchen — cooking appliance, refrigerator, sink, counters and cabinets.\n• Bathroom — toilet, tub or shower, sink, and ventilation.\n• Structural and surfaces — walls, floors, ceilings, interior stairs and their handrails.\n• Safety devices — smoke alarms, carbon monoxide alarms.\n• Doors and windows — including egress, locks, and the unit entry door.\n• HVAC — the heating and cooling that serves the unit.\n• Environmental — mold-like substance, infestation, sharp edges, trip hazards inside the unit.\n\nTHE BOUNDARY CASES THAT PEOPLE GET WRONG\n\nA unit's exterior entry door. The door assembly as it presents to the outside is part of the building exterior, which is an Outside component. What matters for your notes is that the unit entry door standard — the one HUD elevated to Life-Threatening — is about the door serving the dwelling. When you are unsure, record the physical location precisely and the standard name, and resolve the area assignment against the standard rather than by instinct.\n\nA balcony attached to one unit. Attached balconies, porches, patios and carports are listed as Outside components, even though only one household uses the balcony. Residents' intuition (\"it's my balcony\") is not the test.\n\nA window air conditioner installed in a bedroom window. This one has two separate consequences. If it is permanently installed in a window that serves as a means of egress, it is a blocked egress deficiency, and blocked egress is Life-Threatening. That is a Unit finding.\n\nA water heater in a hall closet serving one unit versus a boiler room serving the building. The first is a Unit finding; the second is Inside. Same equipment class, different area, different point weight.\n\nThe resident's own property. A deficiency is still a deficiency when the resident caused it. Furniture blocking a bedroom door or an exterior door is a deficiency even though the furniture is resident-owned. You will need to prepare clients for this conversation, because it is one of the most common sources of \"that isn't our fault\" pushback — and it is also one of the easiest things to fix before an inspection with nothing more than a resident notice and a walk-through."},{"heading":"Area 2 — Inside: common areas and building systems","body":"Inside covers everything within the building envelope that is not a dwelling unit. If a resident can be there, or if building equipment lives there, and it is under roof and not in someone's home, it is Inside.\n\nWhat Inside typically contains:\n• Circulation — corridors, lobbies, vestibules, interior stairwells and their handrails and guardrails, elevators and elevator lobbies.\n• Amenity and program space — community rooms, computer rooms, fitness rooms, management offices, mail rooms.\n• Service space — laundry rooms, trash rooms and chutes, janitorial closets, storage rooms.\n• Building systems space — boiler and mechanical rooms, electrical rooms and main panels, sprinkler riser rooms, fire alarm control panels, elevator machine rooms.\n• Common-area life safety — fire-labeled doors, sprinkler heads and assemblies, fire extinguishers, fire alarm devices, emergency and exit lighting, common-area smoke and CO alarms.\n\nInside is where the largest single-item findings hide, and where the money is. A boiler room with combustibles stacked against the appliance, a fire door propped open with a wedge, a sprinkler head with storage inside 18 inches, an electrical room panel with a missing breaker leaving an open port — each of these is a single observation, made in one room, by one inspector, that will be written up. None of them requires walking a single unit.\n\nThat has a direct consequence for how you sequence a pre-inspection: walk the Inside areas FIRST. They are fast, they are entirely under management's control (no resident scheduling), the fixes are usually cheap, and the same conditions recur at nearly every property. A consultant who spends the first ninety minutes in mechanical rooms, stairwells and the trash room routinely finds more correctable points per hour than in any unit.\n\nNote also that some Inside conditions can be cited repeatedly across the property in a way that Unit conditions cannot. Repeat deficiencies within the same inspectable area are counted once for scoring — so five identical fire-door findings in the Inside area do not multiply into five Inside deductions. Chapter 4 covers the duplicate rule precisely, because misapplying it is the single most common arithmetic error in amateur score projections."},{"heading":"Area 3 — Outside: the site and the building envelope","body":"Outside covers the exterior of the building and everything on the grounds.\n\nBuilding exterior components: roofs and roof drainage, exterior walls and cladding, foundations, exterior doors and windows, attached porches, patios, balconies, carports, fire escapes, and exterior lighting mounted on the building.\n\nSite components: walkways and sidewalks, parking lots and driveways, roads, storm drainage, site and pole lighting, fencing and gates, retaining walls, mailboxes, project signs, refuse disposal areas and enclosures, play areas and playground equipment, detached garages, and non-dwelling buildings.\n\nOutside carries the lowest point weight of the three areas at every severity level, which leads to a predictable and expensive mistake: property managers spend their pre-inspection budget on the parts of the property they can see from the parking lot. Landscaping, fresh paint on the sign, sealcoating. Almost none of it moves the score.\n\nThat does NOT make Outside irrelevant, for three reasons:\n\n1. Life-Threatening deficiencies exist outside and they are still Life-Threatening. A missing guardrail at an elevated walkway, a collapsing stair, an exposed conductor at an exterior fixture, a sinkhole in a walkway — these are severe safety findings that happen to sit in the lowest-weighted area. They still carry a 24-hour correction clock and they still deduct.\n\n2. Outside findings are cheap to fix and therefore excellent points-per-dollar. A 3/4-inch sidewalk lift ground down costs very little. A burned-out pole light is a lamp. The individual deduction is small, but so is the cost, and the ratio is often better than in-unit work.\n\n3. Outside conditions are the leading indicator an inspector uses to calibrate. Inspectors are human. A site that is obviously maintained sets a different tone for the day than one that is not. This is not scored and you should never sell it as if it were — but it is real, and it costs almost nothing.\n\nWhere Outside findings genuinely do move a score: systemic building-envelope problems. A failing roof produces water intrusion, which produces mold-like substance in units, which is scored in the highest-weighted area at a severity that escalates with the affected square footage. When you find a roof problem, you are usually looking at the root of several Unit findings, and that is how you should present it to the client — as one repair that retires a cluster of deductions."},{"heading":"Proficiency check — sort these eight findings by area","body":"Read each description, decide the inspectable area, and then check yourself. Cover the answers first. If you miss more than one, re-read the boundary cases in the Unit section.\n\n1. A missing cover plate on a switch in a second-floor corridor of a mid-rise building.\n2. A permanently installed window air conditioner in a bedroom window of unit 204.\n3. A guardrail missing along a second-story exterior walkway serving four units.\n4. Storage boxes stacked within 12 inches of a sprinkler head in the ground-floor laundry room.\n5. A 1-inch vertical lift in the concrete sidewalk between the parking lot and building B.\n6. Mold-like substance covering roughly 2 square feet on the bathroom ceiling of unit 112.\n7. A fire-labeled stairwell door held open with a rubber wedge.\n8. A water heater in the hall closet of unit 305 whose TPR discharge pipe terminates 9 inches above the floor drain.\n\nANSWERS\n\n1. INSIDE. A corridor is common area within the building envelope. The standard is Electrical — Conductor, Outlet, and Switch; a missing cover plate is the classic exposed-conductor citation.\n2. UNIT. The finding is blocked egress in a dwelling unit. A permanently installed window AC in a window that serves as a means of egress is cited as blocked egress, and blocked egress is Life-Threatening.\n3. OUTSIDE. An exterior walkway is a site and building-exterior component. Note that guardrails are one of the six affirmative requirements not scored until October 1, 2026 — still cited, still corrected, no points until that date.\n4. INSIDE. A laundry room is common area. The sprinkler standard treats an obstruction within 18 inches of the head as a deficiency; 12 inches of stacked boxes is inside that limit and is not one of the built-environment exceptions.\n5. OUTSIDE. Walkways are a site component. A 1-inch vertical differential exceeds the 3/4-inch trip hazard threshold.\n6. UNIT. Interior of a dwelling. At roughly 2 square feet cumulative in one room, this lands in the 1-to-9-square-foot band, which is Severe — and it is in the highest-weighted area.\n7. INSIDE. An interior stairwell door is common area. Fire-labeled doors are also in the six affirmative requirements deferred from scoring until October 1, 2026, but a door held open is a condition finding you should still expect to see cited and corrected.\n8. UNIT. The water heater serves one dwelling and sits inside it. The TPR discharge should terminate between 2 and 6 inches above the flood level of the receptor; 9 inches is outside the allowed range and is a deficiency.\n\nThe pattern worth extracting: five of these eight are things a manager would describe as \"outside the apartment\" in casual conversation, yet three of the five are Unit findings. The area is determined by where the standard applies, not by whose door it is behind."}]},{"id":"ch3","unit":"u1","number":3,"title":"The 4 Severity Levels","subtitle":"HUD's actual definitions, the correction clocks, the escalation rules that change a severity mid-observation, and a sorting drill","sections":[{"heading":"HUD's four definitions, in HUD's own terms","body":"Learn these as written. When you argue a severity call with a client or in a technical review request, you are arguing about whether an observed condition fits one of these four descriptions.\n\nLIFE-THREATENING (LT)\nDeficiencies that, if evident in the home or on the property, present a HIGH RISK OF DEATH to a resident.\n\nSEVERE\nDeficiencies that present a HIGH RISK OF PERMANENT DISABILITY, or SERIOUS INJURY OR ILLNESS, to a resident; or where the PHYSICAL SECURITY OR SAFETY of a resident or their property would be SERIOUSLY COMPROMISED.\n\nMODERATE\nDeficiencies that present a MODERATE RISK of an adverse medical event requiring a healthcare visit, causing temporary harm, or — if left untreated — causing or worsening a chronic condition.\n\nLOW\nDeficiencies that are CRITICAL TO HABITABILITY but that do not present a substantive health or safety risk.\n\nThree things to notice, because they explain most severity calls that surprise people:\n\nFirst, the Severe definition includes SECURITY, not just injury. That is why a non-functioning entry door lock, a broken window latch on an accessible ground-floor unit, or a compromised exterior door sit at Severe. Nobody is going to be injured by a bad deadbolt; the resident's physical security is nonetheless seriously compromised. Consultants who reason only about physical injury consistently under-rate security findings.\n\nSecond, the Moderate definition is about a healthcare visit or a chronic condition, not about inconvenience. This is why a bathroom with no working ventilation is Moderate rather than Low: sustained moisture drives respiratory problems over time. A cabinet door off its hinge is not Moderate, because no medical pathway exists.\n\nThird, Low is not \"minor.\" Low is \"critical to habitability.\" That phrase does real work. Low is reserved for things a home genuinely needs in order to be a home, where the failure carries no substantive health or safety risk. A cosmetic blemish with no habitability consequence is generally below the recording threshold entirely, not a Low.\n\nBELOW THE THRESHOLD\nSeveral standards define a de minimis level below which nothing is recorded at all. The clearest example is mold-like substance: patches totaling less than 4 square inches in a room are below de minimis and are not recorded as a deficiency. Knowing where those floors sit is worth money to a client, because it prevents them from spending on conditions that were never going to be written up."},{"heading":"The correction clocks — and why you must read them off the standard","body":"Severity implies a correction timeframe. The general pattern for the scored portfolios (Public Housing and project-based Multifamily) is:\n\n• Life-Threatening — 24 hours\n• Severe — 30 days\n• Moderate — 30 days\n• Low — 60 days\n\nFor the pass/fail voucher and CPD programs the pattern is tighter at the top: Life-Threatening and Severe deficiencies are generally required to be corrected within 24 hours, Moderate within 30 days, and Low deficiencies are recorded but generally do not fail the unit and carry no required correction timeframe.\n\nNow the part that matters more than the pattern: THE AUTHORITATIVE TIMEFRAME IS PRINTED ON THE STANDARD. Each NSPIRE standard document carries its own correction timeframe, and HUD sets them per program column. A handful of deficiencies do not follow the default for their tier. This is why every experienced consultant works from the standards documents rather than from a memorized table, and why the deliverable you hand a client should cite the standard name next to every line item.\n\nThe 24-hour clock, in operational terms. When a Life-Threatening deficiency is identified:\n• The clock starts when the deficiency is identified, not when the report is issued and not when the owner reads it.\n• Twenty-four hours means twenty-four hours, including nights and weekends. There is no business-day grace.\n• The correction must be real. An interim measure can be acceptable where HUD or its designee permits it — a blank cover plate over a missing GFCI receptacle is the standard example — but an interim repair still has to be followed by a full repair within a reasonable timeframe approved by HUD or its designee. Taping over a hazard is not a correction.\n• Documentation of the correction is what protects the owner later. Photograph before, photograph after, timestamp both, keep the invoice.\n\nWhy this drives your service design. A property manager cannot mobilize an electrician, a glazier and a pest contractor inside 24 hours at 4:00 p.m. on a Friday if they have not pre-arranged it. The single most valuable thing you can build for a client before an inspection is a 24-hour response protocol: named vendors per trade, after-hours numbers, a purchase-authority letter so a supervisor can spend without waiting for approval, and a photo-documentation template. It takes an afternoon to assemble and it is the difference between an uneventful correction and an enforcement problem."},{"heading":"Severity escalation — when the same defect changes tier","body":"Severity is not always fixed per standard. Several standards escalate by MEASUREMENT, by EXTENT, or by LOCATION. These escalation rules are where a working consultant earns the fee, because they are the difference between a small deduction and a property-threatening one — and they are almost always resolvable with a tape measure.\n\nESCALATION BY MEASURED AREA — mold-like substance\nCumulative patches per room:\n• Under 4 square inches — below de minimis, not recorded.\n• More than 4 square inches and less than 1 square foot — Moderate.\n• 1 to 9 square feet — Severe.\n• More than 9 square feet — Life-Threatening.\nOne standard, four different outcomes, determined entirely by an area you can measure. A client who cleans a 10-square-foot wall down to 8 square feet has not fixed anything real, but has moved the finding from Life-Threatening to Severe — which is exactly why the correct advice is to remediate the moisture source, not the visible patch.\n\nESCALATION BY EXTENT — infestation\nA single sighting of a pest such as a cockroach or rodent within a unit or common area is generally handled as a Moderate condition on a 30-day clock. Infestation observed across multiple units or common areas escalates to a Life-Threatening finding requiring correction within 24 hours. Within a single unit, the standard also gives you extent markers: more than three live cockroaches observed simultaneously during daylight, or at least one cockroach in more than one location within the unit, are signs of extensive infestation.\nNote also that NSPIRE does not require live pests. Evidence is sufficient: droppings, shed skins, dead insects, and egg cases (brown oblong cases, roughly 5 to 9 millimeters long) all support a citation.\n\nESCALATION BY LOCATION — the same physical defect in different places\nAn exposed conductor is an exposed conductor whether it is in a unit, a corridor or on an exterior fixture. Severity may be the same, but the Defect Impact Weight is not, and only the in-unit instance counts toward the Unit Threshold.\n\nDE-ESCALATION IS ALSO REAL\nSome conditions that look alarming are explicitly excluded. A sprinkler head with less than 18 inches of clearance is a deficiency — unless the obstruction is a permanent feature of the built environment such as a closet, wall-mounted kitchen cabinet, permanently installed light fixture or exit sign, and a parked vehicle within 18 inches is likewise not the deficiency. Knowing the exclusions stops you from putting items on a punch list that were never going to be cited.\n\nThe professional habit: for every finding, ask \"is there a measurement or an extent that changes this tier?\" If yes, measure it, photograph the measurement, and write the number down. A number in your report is worth ten adjectives."},{"heading":"The Health and Safety Report and the deficiencies you cannot ignore","body":"Alongside the scored inspection report, the inspection produces a Health and Safety Report — the record of health and safety deficiencies found, which is what drives the correction obligations and the notifications to the owner or PHA. It exists because the score and the safety obligations are two different things and HUD does not want them conflated.\n\nThis distinction is worth explaining to every client, because it resolves the most dangerous misconception in the program:\n\nA GOOD SCORE DOES NOT DISCHARGE THE CORRECTION OBLIGATION.\n\nA 400-unit property can absorb a Life-Threatening deficiency in a single sampled unit with a small numeric effect on its score, because the deduction is divided by the unit sample. The 24-hour correction requirement is completely unaffected by that arithmetic. Owners who think in score terms sometimes conclude that a finding \"didn't really cost us anything.\" It cost them a 24-hour clock, a documentation obligation, and potential enforcement exposure if missed.\n\nThe inverse misconception is just as common and just as wrong: a small property owner who corrects a Life-Threatening item within an hour sometimes assumes the deduction goes away. It does not. The deficiency was observed at the time of inspection and it is scored. Correction discharges the safety obligation; it does not un-write the finding. The mechanism for changing what appears on the FINAL report is the technical review process in Chapter 8, and it applies to a defined set of grounds — inspector or HUD error, conditions beyond the owner's control, modernization work in progress, or conflict with state or local code — not to \"we fixed it fast.\"\n\nWhat the inspection report itself contains, so you know what your client will receive: the scores; building and unit inspection data; occupancy information; a deficiency summary; a breakdown of the units sampled; images of any certificates provided to the inspector; detail on Low, Moderate, Severe and Life-Threatening deficiencies; and the Health and Safety Report. Chapter 16 walks a report end to end."},{"heading":"Proficiency check — sort these eight findings by severity","body":"For each, name the severity tier and the reasoning. Cover the answers first. Aim for 7 of 8; if you miss the security-based ones, re-read the Severe definition.\n\n1. In unit 108, the deadbolt on the entry door is broken and the door cannot be locked.\n2. In unit 214, cumulative mold-like patches on the bathroom ceiling measure roughly 14 square feet.\n3. In unit 302, the bathroom has no exhaust fan, no passive vent, and no operable window.\n4. In the second-floor corridor, a junction box has a missing knockout leaving a 1-inch opening with conductors visible.\n5. Across six sampled units and the laundry room, cockroach droppings and shed skins are present, with live roaches seen in three of the units.\n6. In unit 117, one kitchen cabinet door is off its lower hinge and hangs crooked. Nothing sharp is exposed.\n7. In unit 405, a permanently installed window air conditioner fills the bedroom's only egress window.\n8. On the bathroom wall of unit 221, a patch of mold-like substance measures about 3 inches by 1 inch.\n\nANSWERS\n\n1. SEVERE. Not because anyone will be injured, but because the resident's physical security is seriously compromised — which is written into the Severe definition. This is the call people most often get wrong by reasoning only about injury.\n2. LIFE-THREATENING. More than 9 square feet cumulative in a room crosses the top mold threshold. Twenty-four-hour clock, and the real fix is the moisture source.\n3. MODERATE. NSPIRE accepts a mechanical exhaust fan, a passive vent, or an operable window. With none of the three present, the absence of ventilation is a Moderate finding — the medical pathway is sustained moisture and respiratory effect over time, not immediate harm.\n4. Exposed conductor. The standard treats an opening or gap greater than 1/2 inch that leaves a conductor unenclosed as the deficiency, and a 1-inch missing knockout with visible conductors is squarely inside it. Treat it as a top-tier electrical hazard on a 24-hour clock and confirm the tier against the current standard document for the program in question.\n5. LIFE-THREATENING. Infestation observed across multiple units and common areas escalates to the top tier with a 24-hour clock. Note that the droppings and shed skins alone would have supported citation even with no live insects seen.\n6. Below the recording threshold, or at most Low. There is no health or safety pathway and nothing here is critical to habitability. Advise the client to fix it because residents notice — but do not put points against it.\n7. LIFE-THREATENING. Blocked egress. A permanently installed window unit in a window serving as a means of egress is the textbook citation, and blocked egress is Life-Threatening.\n8. NOT RECORDED. Three square inches is under the 4-square-inch de minimis floor for mold-like substance. Still worth mentioning to the client as a moisture warning — but it is not a deficiency and it should not appear on a projected score.\n\nScoring yourself: items 1, 3 and 6 are the discriminating ones. Item 1 tests whether you internalized security. Item 3 tests whether you can distinguish a chronic-condition pathway from mere inconvenience. Item 6 tests whether you can decline to cite something."},{"heading":"Building your own severity reference","body":"You will not memorize every standard, and you should not try. Build a working reference instead. Here is the structure that survives contact with a real inspection day.\n\nONE ROW PER STANDARD, six columns:\n1. Standard name exactly as HUD publishes it (Guardrail; Mold-like Substance; Electrical — Conductor, Outlet, and Switch; Water Heater; Trip Hazard; and so on).\n2. Which areas it applies to (Unit, Inside, Outside, or a combination).\n3. The specific deficiency text you most often observe.\n4. The measurable criterion, written as a number (3/4 inch; 18 inches; 4 square inches / 1 square foot / 9 square feet; 6 feet from water; 2 to 6 inches above flood level; 3 feet from an ignition source; 4 or more risers; 30 inches above grade).\n5. Severity, and any escalation rule.\n6. Correction timeframe as printed on the standard, per program.\n\nThen add two flags that will make you faster than most of your competition:\n• AFFIRMATIVE-REQUIREMENT flag, for the six categories not scored until October 1, 2026 — fire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI, guardrail, HVAC, interior lighting, and minimum electrical and lighting. You will be handling these constantly in the run-up to that date and you need to be able to say \"cited, corrected, not scored yet\" without hesitating.\n• EXCLUSION flag, for the conditions the standard explicitly carves out — the built-environment exceptions to the 18-inch sprinkler rule, the fire-extinguisher tag exception when a servicing company's invoice or report dated within the past year is available, and the fact that the elevator certificate is verified during the inspection but neither its absence nor its expiration is itself a recorded deficiency.\n\nCarry it on a tablet, sorted by area, and update it whenever HUD revises a standard. The standards are versioned and they do change; a reference built once and never revisited becomes a liability. Two habits keep it honest: check the standards for revisions before every engagement, and write the version or revision date at the top of your own reference so a client can see how current it is.\n\nThe next three chapters turn these severity calls into numbers. Chapter 4 gives you the scoring mechanics, Chapter 5 the Life-Threatening catalogue, and Chapter 6 the Severe, Moderate and Low catalogue."}]},{"id":"ch4","unit":"u2","number":4,"title":"Scoring Mechanics","subtitle":"The formula, the two thresholds, the duplicate rule, the sample divisor — and six worked calculations","sections":[{"heading":"The formula, stated once and used forever","body":"Every NSPIRE score for a scored property comes out of one calculation:\n\nSCORE = 100 minus the sum, over every recorded deficiency, of\n        (Defect Impact Weight) x (number of that deficiency) divided by (number of units in the inspected sample)\n\nThat is the whole model. Three inputs: a weight that depends on the severity and the inspectable area, a count, and a divisor.\n\nTHE DEFECT IMPACT WEIGHT TABLE\n\n                    Unit      Inside    Outside\nLife-Threatening    60.0      54.5      49.6\nSevere              40.0      36.4      33.1\nModerate            20.0      18.2      16.5\nLow                  2.4       2.2       2.0\n\nRead the table twice and notice two structural facts that will make you fast:\n\n1. THE AREA SPREAD IS NARROW. Inside is roughly 91 percent of Unit, and Outside roughly 83 percent of Unit, at every severity level. Moving a deficiency from a unit to a corridor saves you about 9 percent of the deduction. This surprises people who assume \"Outside barely counts.\" It counts almost as much per finding; what makes Outside cheap is that Outside findings are usually cheap to fix, not that they are lightly weighted.\n\n2. THE SEVERITY SPREAD IS ENORMOUS. Within the Unit column, Low is 2.4 and Life-Threatening is 60.0 — a factor of 25. One Life-Threatening finding outweighs twenty-five Low findings. Severity, not location, is what drives a score.\n\nCombine those two observations and you have the master heuristic of this entire business: WORK THE SEVERITY LADDER, NOT THE MAP. A consultant who chases every Low item across a 300-unit site while leaving two Severe items in sampled units has inverted the arithmetic.\n\nBefore you build a client deliverable on these numbers, confirm the current table against HUD's published NSPIRE scoring notice for the inspection year in question. HUD has revised scoring guidance more than once, and a table that was right last year is a liability this year. That verification takes ten minutes and it is part of what the client is paying for."},{"heading":"The divisor: why property size changes everything","body":"The denominator in the formula is the number of units in the inspected SAMPLE, not the number of units at the property. This single fact explains most of what feels counterintuitive about NSPIRE scores.\n\nHOW THE SAMPLE IS SET\nHUD draws a statistical sample sized to reach a 90 percent confidence level with a 6 percent margin of error. The sample grows with property size, but far more slowly than the property does, and it is capped. Under NSPIRE the maximum number of units in the scoring sample is 32 — raised from the 27-unit maximum that applied under UPCS. Very small properties can have all or nearly all units inspected.\n\nDo not memorize a sample-size table from a third-party source. Sample size is set by HUD's methodology for the specific inspection, and the number that matters is the one printed on the property's own report. Ask the client for the prior report, read the sample size off it, and use that. If there is no prior report, run your projection at two or three plausible sample sizes and give the client a range — that is more honest and more useful than a single false-precision number.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE 1 — the same defect at three property sizes\nOne Life-Threatening deficiency in one sampled unit. Weight 60.0.\n\n• 12-unit property, sample of 8: 60.0 / 8 = 7.50 points. Score 92.50.\n• 100-unit property, sample of 20: 60.0 / 20 = 3.00 points. Score 97.00.\n• 300-unit property, sample of 32: 60.0 / 32 = 1.88 points. Score 98.13.\n\nIdentical condition, identical severity, identical 24-hour correction obligation — and a four-fold difference in score impact.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS IN PRACTICE\n\nFor SMALL properties: every single finding is expensive. At a sample of 8, three Severe unit findings cost 40.0 x 3 / 8 = 15.00 points. A small property has no statistical cushion, so a pre-inspection service is close to essential, and the client should understand that a couple of bad units can carry the whole score.\n\nFor LARGE properties: individual findings are cheap, but there is a trap. The sample is drawn from the whole property, so a systemic condition — the same missing GFCI in every kitchen, the same unventilated bathroom in every unit of a given floor plan — will appear in a large share of the sampled units. Systemic conditions defeat the statistical cushion completely, because they are present wherever the sample lands.\n\nThat is the correct way to explain risk to a large-property owner: you are not exposed to your worst unit, you are exposed to your worst REPEATED condition. Find the conditions that repeat by floor plan, by building, or by construction era, and you have found what will actually move the score."},{"heading":"The two thresholds — and the trap that fails good-looking properties","body":"A scored property must clear TWO independent tests. Passing one does not help you with the other.\n\nTEST 1 — THE PROPERTY THRESHOLD OF PERFORMANCE\nThe overall score must be 60 or above. Below 60 is a failure.\n\nTEST 2 — THE UNIT THRESHOLD OF PERFORMANCE\nDeductions arising from the UNIT inspectable area are totalled separately. If 30 or more points are deducted from the Unit portion, the score is adjusted to 59 — a failure — even if the Inside and Outside portions would have carried the property above 60.\n\nThere is no equivalent threshold for Inside or Outside. The asymmetry is deliberate: it is the mechanism by which NSPIRE forces attention onto the conditions residents actually live in.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE 2 — the Unit Threshold trap\nA 14-unit property, sample of 10. Findings, all in the Unit area:\n• 2 Life-Threatening (two different standards): 60.0 x 2 = 120.0\n• 4 Severe (four different standards): 40.0 x 4 = 160.0\n• 3 Moderate (three different standards): 20.0 x 3 = 60.0\nUnit total weight = 340.0. Divided by the sample of 10 = 34.00 points of Unit deduction.\n\nInside and Outside: clean. Nothing found.\n\nRaw score: 100 - 34.00 = 66.00. That looks like a comfortable pass.\nUnit Threshold test: 34.00 is at or above 30. The score is adjusted to 59.\n\nTHIS PROPERTY FAILS AT 59 WITH A RAW SCORE OF 66.\n\nThis is the scenario that blindsides owners, and it is the single most valuable thing you can catch before an inspection. Run the Unit Threshold test SEPARATELY on every projection you produce. Not as a footnote — as a headline number next to the projected score.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE 3 — both tests failed at once\nA 60-unit property, sample of 20:\n• Unit: 4 LT (240.0), 6 Severe (240.0), 8 Moderate (160.0) = 640.0 / 20 = 32.00 points\n• Inside: 1 LT (54.5), 3 Severe (109.2) = 163.7 / 20 = 8.19 points\n• Outside: 5 Moderate (82.5) = 82.5 / 20 = 4.13 points\nTotal deduction 44.31. Raw score 55.69 — already below 60, so the Property Threshold fails.\nUnit deduction 32.00 — at or above 30, so the Unit Threshold fails too.\n\nWhy it matters that BOTH failed: the remediation plans are different. To clear the Property Threshold you can harvest points anywhere, including cheap Inside and Outside work. To clear the Unit Threshold you must reduce Unit-area deductions specifically — Inside and Outside repairs do nothing for it. A recovery plan that ignores this will spend money and still fail. Chapter 17 walks a full recovery build.\n\nThe arithmetic of clearing the Unit Threshold in Example 3: Unit deductions must drop below 30, so you need to shed more than 2.00 points of Unit deduction, which at a sample of 20 means shedding more than 40.0 of Unit weight — exactly one Severe unit finding, or one Life-Threatening finding with room to spare. Knowing that lets you tell the client \"one specific repair moves you off the Unit Threshold\" instead of \"fix everything.\""},{"heading":"The duplicate rule — the most common arithmetic error","body":"Repeated deficiencies within the SAME inspectable area are counted ONCE for scoring purposes.\n\nThat single sentence is responsible for more wrong projections than anything else in the program, in both directions. People either ignore it entirely and multiply every instance, or they over-apply it and collapse genuinely distinct findings.\n\nWHAT THE RULE DOES COVER\nThe same deficiency, under the same standard, observed repeatedly within one inspectable area, is scored once for that area.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT DO\nIt does not merge DIFFERENT standards. A missing outlet cover and a missing GFCI are two different deficiencies and both count.\nIt does not merge across AREAS. A finding in the Unit area and the same finding in the Inside area are separate; they carry different weights and only the Unit one feeds the Unit Threshold.\nIt does not discharge the CORRECTION obligation for the instances that were not scored. Every instance still has to be fixed. Scoring once is a scoring convention, not an amnesty.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE 4 — applying the rule correctly\nA 40-unit property, sample of 16. The inspector records:\n• Missing outlet cover plate in 6 different sampled units (same standard, Unit area)\n• Missing outlet cover plate in 2 corridors (same standard, Inside area)\n• Inoperable bathroom exhaust with no window or passive vent in 4 sampled units (different standard, Unit area)\n• Trip hazard, 7 locations along the site walkways (same standard, Outside area)\n\nApplying the rule:\n• Cover plates in units: one Unit-area instance scored.\n• Cover plates in corridors: one Inside-area instance scored — separate from the Unit one, because the area differs.\n• Bathroom ventilation: a different standard, so it scores on its own, once for the Unit area.\n• Trip hazards: seven observations, one Outside-area instance scored.\n\nThe client's instinct will be that 19 observations means 19 deductions. It does not. But all 19 conditions still have to be corrected, on their own severity clocks.\n\nWHY THIS CHANGES YOUR ADVICE\nThe duplicate rule inverts the intuitive repair priority for widespread conditions. If a property has one Severe finding in a single unit and the same Severe finding in twenty units, the SCORE consequence is identical — one Unit-area instance either way. So a client who fixes nineteen of the twenty has bought nothing at all in score terms; only fixing the twentieth removes the deduction.\n\nThat leads to two pieces of advice you should be ready to give, in this order:\n1. For score purposes, a widespread condition must be eliminated COMPLETELY from the area to remove the deduction. Partial remediation of a repeated deficiency buys zero points.\n2. For safety and compliance purposes, partial remediation still matters enormously, because every instance carries its own correction obligation and its own liability. Never let a client hear \"it only scores once\" as \"we only have to fix one.\"\n\nA caution on how far to push the rule: work it against the property's actual prior report where one exists. The scored line items on a real report are the ground truth for how HUD applied the convention to that property, and they will teach you more in twenty minutes than any secondary summary."},{"heading":"Worked example 5 — a full property, start to finish","body":"A 100-unit project-based Multifamily property. Prior report shows a sample of 20 units. You walk it and record the following, already de-duplicated by standard within each area.\n\nUNIT AREA\n• 1 Life-Threatening: exposed conductor at a damaged range receptacle. 60.0 x 1 = 60.0\n• 3 Severe (three different standards): entry door will not lock; water heater TPR discharge terminates 11 inches above the floor drain; mold-like substance measuring about 4 square feet in a bathroom. 40.0 x 3 = 120.0\n• 5 Moderate (five different standards): no bathroom ventilation; inoperable range burner; damaged floor covering creating a hazard; leaking supply connection under a kitchen sink; damaged interior door. 20.0 x 5 = 100.0\n• 2 Low (two different standards). 2.4 x 2 = 4.8\nUnit weight total = 284.8. Unit deduction = 284.8 / 20 = 14.24 points.\n\nINSIDE AREA\n• 1 Severe: sprinkler head obstructed by stored material within 18 inches in the laundry room. 36.4\n• 2 Moderate (two different standards): damaged corridor flooring; inoperable emergency light. 18.2 x 2 = 36.4\nInside weight total = 72.8. Inside deduction = 72.8 / 20 = 3.64 points.\n\nOUTSIDE AREA\n• 1 Moderate: deteriorated walkway surface. 16.5\n• 3 Low (three different standards). 2.0 x 3 = 6.0\nOutside weight total = 22.5. Outside deduction = 22.5 / 20 = 1.13 points.\n\nRESULT\nTotal deduction = 14.24 + 3.64 + 1.13 = 19.01\nProjected score = 100 - 19.01 = 80.99, call it 81.\nUnit Threshold test: 14.24, well under 30. Not triggered.\nProperty Threshold test: 81 is above 60. Pass.\n\nInspection cycle consequence: a score in the 80s puts this property on a two-year cycle rather than the three-year cycle available at 90 and above. That is the argument for the next tier of work, and it is worth real money to an owner — a two-year cycle means an extra inspection event, extra preparation cost, and extra exposure over any six-year window.\n\nWHERE THE NEXT NINE POINTS COME FROM\nTo reach 90 the property needs to shed about 9.0 points of deduction, which at a sample of 20 means shedding about 180 of weight. Ranked by weight recovered:\n• The single Life-Threatening exposed conductor: 60.0 of weight, 3.00 points, and the cheapest repair on the list. Do it first, and note that it has a 24-hour clock anyway.\n• The three Severe unit findings: 120.0 of weight, 6.00 points. The door lock is inexpensive; the TPR discharge pipe is a short plumbing call; the mold requires finding and fixing the moisture source, which is the expensive one.\n• Those four items alone total 9.00 points and land the property at 90.\n\nNotice what did not make the list: all eight Moderate and Low unit findings together are worth 104.8 of weight, or 5.24 points, and represent far more labor than the four items above. And every Outside item combined is worth 1.13 points. THIS is the deliverable a client cannot produce internally — not a list of what is wrong, but a ranked list of what is worth fixing first and why."},{"heading":"Worked example 6 — the small property that fails on paper","body":"A 24-unit Public Housing property, sample of 12. This is the profile where NSPIRE bites hardest, and it is worth walking in full because nothing about it looks catastrophic on the ground.\n\nUNIT AREA\n• 1 Life-Threatening: blocked egress, a permanently installed window AC in a bedroom's only egress window. 60.0\n• 2 Severe: broken entry door lock; mold-like substance about 2 square feet. 40.0 x 2 = 80.0\n• 6 Moderate (six different standards). 20.0 x 6 = 120.0\n• 4 Low (four different standards). 2.4 x 4 = 9.6\nUnit weight = 269.6. Unit deduction = 269.6 / 12 = 22.47 points.\n\nINSIDE AREA\n• 1 Life-Threatening: exposed conductor at an open panel port in the electrical room. 54.5\n• 2 Severe: fire extinguisher tag expired more than a year; combustibles stored within three feet of the boiler. 36.4 x 2 = 72.8\n• 3 Moderate. 18.2 x 3 = 54.6\n• 5 Low. 2.2 x 5 = 11.0\nInside weight = 192.9. Inside deduction = 192.9 / 12 = 16.08 points.\n\nOUTSIDE AREA\n• 1 Severe: 33.1\n• 4 Moderate: 16.5 x 4 = 66.0\n• 6 Low: 2.0 x 6 = 12.0\nOutside weight = 111.1. Outside deduction = 111.1 / 12 = 9.26 points.\n\nRESULT\nTotal deduction = 22.47 + 16.08 + 9.26 = 47.80\nScore = 100 - 47.80 = 52.20. FAIL on the Property Threshold.\nUnit Threshold: 22.47, under 30. Not triggered.\n\nREAD THE STRUCTURE OF THE FAILURE. This property did not fail because of its units. Unit deductions were 22.47 of the 47.80. Inside and Outside together contributed 25.33 — more than half the total damage — from conditions that are, almost without exception, cheap and entirely under management's control: an open panel port, an expired extinguisher tag, boxes near a boiler, some walkway and site items.\n\nThe recovery plan writes itself:\n• Clear the Inside Life-Threatening exposed conductor and both Inside Severe items: 54.5 + 72.8 = 127.3 of weight, or 10.61 points. Cost: a panel cover, an extinguisher service call, and an hour of moving boxes.\n• Clear the Outside Severe and all four Outside Moderate items: 33.1 + 66.0 = 99.1 of weight, or 8.26 points.\n• Those two moves alone take the score from 52.2 to 71.1 — from a clear failure to a comfortable pass — before touching a single dwelling unit.\n\nThe lesson to carry into every engagement: the intuition that \"units are weighted most, so work the units\" is right about the WEIGHT and wrong about the WORK. Weight per finding is only one factor. The other two are how many distinct findings exist in an area and what each one costs to clear. At most small properties the Inside area is dense with cheap, high-severity findings, and it is where the first day of your engagement belongs."}]},{"id":"ch5","unit":"u2","number":5,"title":"Life-Threatening Deficiencies","subtitle":"The top tier — what qualifies, the exact criteria, the 24-hour clock, and worked scenarios for each family","sections":[{"heading":"Why this tier owns your first day","body":"Life-Threatening deficiencies are defined by HUD as those that present a HIGH RISK OF DEATH to a resident. They carry the heaviest weight in the scoring model — 60.0 in a unit, 54.5 inside, 49.6 outside — and a 24-hour correction clock that runs through nights and weekends.\n\nThree reasons the Life-Threatening list is where a pre-inspection engagement starts:\n\nTHE ARITHMETIC. In the Unit column, one Life-Threatening finding equals one and a half Severe findings, three Moderate findings, or twenty-five Low findings. At a sample of 12, a single LT unit item costs 5.00 points. Nothing else on the property moves a score that fast.\n\nTHE CLOCK. Every other severity gives the owner at least 30 days. Life-Threatening gives them 24 hours from identification, and an owner who has not pre-arranged after-hours vendor access will miss it. Finding these items during a pre-inspection converts an emergency into a scheduled repair.\n\nTHE COST ASYMMETRY. Most Life-Threatening findings are cheap to correct. A cover plate. A lock. A battery-sealed alarm. Removing a window AC unit. The exceptions — a large mold remediation, a structural failure, a gas system repair — are genuinely expensive, but they are the minority. The typical LT punch list is the highest points-per-dollar work on the property by a wide margin.\n\nA note on what is NOT here. HVAC and heating failures sit inside the six affirmative requirements whose SCORING is deferred to October 1, 2026. They are still inspected, still cited, and still require correction on their stated timeframe. They simply do not deduct points before that date. The same is true of guardrails and fire-labeled doors. Treat them as fully live for safety and compliance and as pending for scoring, and say exactly that to the client rather than letting them hear \"not scored\" as \"not required.\""},{"heading":"Family 1 — Electrical hazards","body":"THE CORE STANDARD: Electrical — Conductor, Outlet, and Switch.\nThe deficiency is any exposed electrical conductor that presents a hazard: a conductor that is not enclosed or properly insulated. HUD's listed examples include damaged sheathing, an open port, a missing knockout, a missing outlet or switch cover, a missing breaker or fuse, and a missing lightbulb in a fixture that leaves the socket open. The measurable criterion that decides most calls is AN OPENING OR GAP GREATER THAN 1/2 INCH.\n\nCarry a half-inch reference — the width of your thumb, a coin, a marked spot on your tape. Most disputes about whether an opening counts are settled by measuring it.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 1 — the missing cover plate\nUnit 204, a switch in the hallway is missing its cover plate. The opening is roughly 2 by 3 inches with conductors visible.\nCall: exposed conductor. Well over the 1/2-inch criterion. Weight 60.0 in the Unit area; at a sample of 15 that is 4.00 points. Correction: a plate costs under two dollars and takes ninety seconds.\nConsultant's note: this is the single most common finding in the entire program and the most embarrassing one for a manager to be cited on. Walk every unit for cover plates and do a plate sweep before any inspection.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 2 — the electrical panel with an open port\nThe building electrical room panel is missing a breaker; the empty position leaves an open port about 1 inch wide with the bus visible.\nCall: exposed conductor, Inside area. Weight 54.5. At a sample of 12, 4.54 points. Correction: a filler plate or a proper breaker.\nConsultant's note: panels are where a single observation costs the most for the least effort to fix. Open every electrical room, every mechanical room, and every panel door on your first pass.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 3 — the exterior fixture\nA wall-pack light on the rear of building C has a cracked housing with the wiring splice visible and weather-exposed.\nCall: exposed conductor, Outside area. Weight 49.6. Note that Outside is only about 17 percent below the Unit weight — this is not a trivial finding just because it is outdoors.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 4 — water meeting electricity\nA supply leak above a bedroom has saturated the ceiling and is dripping onto a light fixture and running down the wall to an outlet.\nCall: this is the intersection the standards treat most seriously — an energized component in contact with water. Treat it as a top-tier hazard, de-energize the circuit immediately, and do not wait for a repair schedule.\nConsultant's note: when you find water and electricity together, your first action is not to write it down, it is to tell someone with the authority to kill the circuit. Then write it down.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 5 — the borderline case\nAn outlet cover is present but cracked, with a hairline split. No conductor is visible and no opening approaches 1/2 inch.\nCall: not an exposed-conductor deficiency under the measurable criterion. Recommend replacement anyway — plates are trivially cheap and a cracked one may be a missing one by the time the inspector arrives — but do not put points against it in your projection. Being able to decline to cite is as professionally valuable as being able to cite."},{"heading":"Family 2 — Gas, carbon monoxide, and smoke alarms","body":"GAS AND FUEL LEAKS\nA natural gas, propane, or oil leak is a listed deficiency and belongs in the top tier. There is no size threshold to argue about; a detectable leak is the deficiency.\nRelated: chimney and flue piping that is blocked, misaligned, or missing — including evidence of prior installation now absent, disconnected pieces, or misalignment at connections along the run — because that is the path by which combustion gases reach residents rather than the exterior. A visually accessible chimney, flue, or firebox connected to a fireplace or wood-burning appliance that is incomplete or damaged such that it may not safely contain fire and convey smoke and combustion gases to the exterior is likewise a deficiency.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 1 — the disconnected flue\nIn a unit's utility closet, the flue pipe from the gas water heater has separated at a joint, leaving a visible gap where the sections should meet.\nCall: flue misaligned or disconnected. Combustion gases are venting into the closet and from there into the dwelling. Immediate action, and this is one where you should ask whether the unit is safe to occupy tonight rather than merely writing a line item.\n\nCARBON MONOXIDE ALARMS\nNSPIRE incorporates the statutory requirement that HUD housing comply with chapters 9 and 11 of the 2018 International Fire Code for carbon monoxide detection, and the final rule requires CO detection in both the Inside area and the Unit. This requirement is statutory and was not part of any of the NSPIRE compliance extensions — it applies regardless of program or transition status.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 2 — the CO alarm that was never installed\nA 1970s building with gas ranges and gas-fired water heaters in each unit has smoke alarms throughout but no carbon monoxide alarms anywhere.\nCall: a portfolio-wide condition. Note carefully how the duplicate rule interacts: within the Unit area this scores once regardless of how many units are missing alarms, and within the Inside area once more. But every single unit still requires an alarm, on a 24-hour clock, and the owner's exposure here is far larger than the score suggests. This is exactly the case where you tell a client \"this is a small number of points and a very large liability.\"\n\nSMOKE ALARMS\nSmoke alarms must be installed in each rental unit in the appropriate locations per NFPA 72 or any successor standard — which in practice means on each level and inside each sleeping area, plus outside sleeping areas. Alarms must be hardwired or have a sealed, tamper-resistant 10-year battery. For units built or substantially rehabilitated after December 2022, hardwired is the only acceptable option.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 3 — the alarm that works but does not comply\nA unit has a working, correctly placed smoke alarm powered by a user-replaceable 9-volt battery, in a building constructed in 1998.\nCall: the alarm functions, but the power-source requirement is not met — it is neither hardwired nor sealed with a 10-year battery. This is the finding that surprises managers most, because the device works when you press the test button. Replacement with sealed 10-year units is inexpensive and should be a standing capital line for any owner who has not done it.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 4 — coverage gaps\nA three-bedroom unit has one alarm in the central hallway and none inside any bedroom.\nCall: placement does not satisfy NFPA 72, which requires alarms inside each sleeping area as well as outside them. Count the sleeping rooms and the levels on every walk; this is a counting exercise, not a judgment call.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 5 — the disabled alarm\nAn alarm in unit 311 has been removed from its base and is sitting on a shelf, and a second alarm has had its battery removed.\nCall: inoperable smoke alarms. That the resident disabled them does not make it a non-deficiency; the obligation runs to the owner. Practical prevention: sealed 10-year units with tamper-resistant mounts remove the nuisance-silencing incentive that causes most of this, and a documented resident notice plus an alarm check during the annual self-inspection creates the paper trail."},{"heading":"Family 3 — Egress and unit entry doors","body":"BLOCKED EGRESS IS ALWAYS TOP TIER. This is one of the clearest rules in NSPIRE and one of the most frequently triggered.\n\nTHE CRITERIA\n• All egress doors must be able to open and close completely and be free of obstructions.\n• Double-key cylinder deadbolts, or any lock requiring a key, tool, or special knowledge or effort to operate from the egress side, are prohibited on any door serving as an exit or along the exit access.\n• Any window usable as a means of egress, primary or otherwise, must be able to open fully. Window locks requiring a key, tool, or special knowledge from the egress side are cited as blocked egress.\n• A permanently installed window-mounted air conditioner in an egress window is cited as blocked egress.\n• Obstructions count even when the obstructing item belongs to the resident. Furniture blocking a bedroom door or an exterior door is a deficiency.\n\nUNIT ENTRY DOORS\nThe severity of unit entry door deficiencies was raised to Life-Threatening — and this applies even where the door is not a fire-labeled door. A unit entry door that is missing, will not close, will not latch, or is damaged such that it cannot perform its function is a top-tier finding.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 1 — the double-cylinder deadbolt\nA resident in a ground-floor unit has installed a deadbolt requiring a key on the inside, and keeps the key on a hook by the door.\nCall: blocked egress. The key on the hook is not a defense; the standard prohibits a lock requiring a key, tool, or special knowledge from the egress side. Correction is a single-cylinder replacement, roughly thirty dollars and twenty minutes.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 2 — the painted-shut bedroom window\nA bedroom window in a unit has been painted shut over several repaint cycles and will not open.\nCall: blocked egress. A window usable as a means of egress must open fully.\nConsultant's note: this is endemic in older properties that have been repainted repeatedly, and it is invisible unless you physically operate every window. Operate every window. Every one.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 3 — the window air conditioner\nUnit 405 has a permanently installed window AC filling the bedroom's only egress window; unit 407 has a portable AC with a hose vented through a window that still opens fully.\nCall: 405 is blocked egress. 407 is not — the window remains operable. The distinction is whether the window can still perform as a means of egress.\nConsultant's note: this is a seasonal finding and a difficult resident conversation. Address it in writing, ahead of the season, with an alternative offered.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 4 — the entry door that will not latch\nUnit 118's entry door has a warped frame; the door closes but the latch does not engage and the door swings back open.\nCall: unit entry door, top tier. Note that a similar-sounding finding — a functioning door with a broken deadbolt — falls under the security-compromise language in the Severe definition rather than here. The distinction is whether the door itself can perform its function.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 5 — the stored-goods corridor\nAn Inside corridor serving as exit access has resident storage — a bicycle and stacked boxes — narrowing the path.\nCall: blocked egress in the Inside area. Weight 54.5. The correction is free: move the items and send a notice. This is the highest-value thirty seconds of work available at most properties, and it recurs constantly, which makes a documented monthly egress walk an easy recurring service to sell."},{"heading":"Family 4 — Mold, infestation, and structural collapse","body":"MOLD-LIKE SUBSTANCE OVER 9 SQUARE FEET\nThe mold standard escalates by cumulative measured area within a room: under 4 square inches is below de minimis and not recorded; more than 4 square inches to under 1 square foot is Moderate; 1 to 9 square feet is Severe; more than 9 square feet is Life-Threatening.\n\nThe standard is about VISUAL observation — regular or irregular patches or spots on surfaces, in colorations including white, green, yellow, gray, brown or black, often appearing fuzzy or cottony. Odor is an indicator but not diagnostic: a musty smell requires visual confirmation before a mold-like substance citation.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 1 — measuring correctly\nA bathroom has staining on the ceiling around the fan, about 18 by 24 inches, plus two patches behind the toilet of roughly 6 by 6 inches each.\nMeasurement: 18 x 24 inches = 432 square inches = 3.0 square feet. Two 6 x 6 patches = 72 square inches = 0.5 square feet. Cumulative in the room: 3.5 square feet.\nCall: the 1-to-9-square-foot band, Severe. Not Life-Threatening. Measure and add; do not eyeball.\nConsultant's note: the remediation advice is the same regardless of tier — find and fix the moisture source. In this scenario the fan is the prime suspect, and an inoperative bathroom exhaust is its own separate Moderate finding under a different standard.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 2 — crossing the top threshold\nA unit with a long-running roof leak has staining and growth across most of a bedroom ceiling, roughly 10 by 12 feet, of which perhaps 10 square feet shows visible mold-like substance.\nCall: over 9 square feet, top tier, 24-hour clock. Note the root cause is an Outside-area roof failure — one repair addressing several Unit-area findings.\n\nINFESTATION\nEvidence alone is sufficient; live pests are not required. Droppings, insect eggs, shed skins, dead insects and strong odors all support a citation. Cockroach evidence specifically includes dead or live insects, shed skins, droppings appearing as small black specks or smears, and egg cases — brown oblong cases roughly 5 to 9 millimeters long.\nExtent markers within a unit: three or fewer live cockroaches observed simultaneously in one location during the day is evidence of cockroaches; MORE than three observed simultaneously during the day indicates extensive infestation, as does at least one cockroach in more than one location within the unit.\nExtent across the property: a single sighting in a unit or common area is generally handled as a Moderate condition on a 30-day clock; pests observed across MULTIPLE units or common areas escalate to the top tier with a 24-hour requirement.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 3 — one unit versus the property\nProperty A: one unit shows two live roaches under the sink and nothing elsewhere. Property B: four sampled units show droppings and shed skins, and the trash room shows live insects.\nCall: A is the Moderate single-sighting case. B is the multiple-units-and-common-areas case and escalates to the top tier.\nConsultant's note: this is the finding most improved by an integrated pest management contract with documented service records, because the documentation supports the technical review argument that the condition was being actively managed.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 4 — evidence with no live pests\nA vacant unit has mouse droppings along the baseboard behind the stove and gnaw marks on a cabinet toe-kick. No live rodents.\nCall: citable. NSPIRE does not require live pests. Clean the evidence AND close the entry points; a client who only cleans will be cited again next year.\n\nSTRUCTURAL COLLAPSE AND FALL HAZARDS\nConditions where a structure, walking surface, stair, or elevated platform presents a genuine collapse or fall risk belong at the top of the severity ladder.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 5 — the exterior stair\nThe wooden exterior stair serving two second-floor units has a stringer split through, and the tread deflects visibly under load.\nCall: a fall and collapse hazard on an Outside-area stair. Weight 49.6, and the correction is immediate — close the stair, provide alternate access, repair. Note separately that a missing guardrail alongside the same stair is a Guardrail finding, which sits in the six affirmative requirements deferred from scoring until October 1, 2026: cited and required to be corrected, but not deducting points before that date."},{"heading":"Building the 24-hour response protocol","body":"This section is the deliverable, not the theory. Every client you take on should end up with this document, and assembling it is billable work that takes one afternoon.\n\n1. VENDOR CARD, BY TRADE\nElectrician, plumber, HVAC, glazier and locksmith, pest control, fire protection (extinguisher and sprinkler service), elevator, and a general handyman. For each: company, primary contact, after-hours number, average response time, and whether they are already set up as an approved vendor in the client's accounting system. That last column is where most 24-hour failures actually originate — not a missing phone number, but a vendor who cannot be paid.\n\n2. SPENDING AUTHORITY LETTER\nA signed letter from the owner or asset manager authorizing the site supervisor to commit up to a stated amount, without prior approval, for a documented Life-Threatening correction. Without it, a Friday-evening finding waits for a Monday-morning approval and the clock expires over the weekend.\n\n3. THE FIVE PRE-STOCKED ITEMS\nBecause these five account for a disproportionate share of Life-Threatening findings and all five are cheap to hold in inventory:\n• Outlet and switch cover plates, and panel filler plates, in the common sizes\n• Sealed 10-year smoke alarms and carbon monoxide alarms\n• Single-cylinder deadbolts and standard entry lock sets\n• Breakers matching the property's panel types\n• Plywood, fasteners and barricade tape for isolating a fall or collapse hazard while a permanent repair is scheduled\n\n4. DOCUMENTATION TEMPLATE\nFor each corrected item: the standard name, the location, a before photograph, an after photograph, the date and time of both, the name of who performed the work, and the invoice or work order number. This is what turns a correction into evidence — and evidence is what a technical review request runs on, per Chapter 8.\n\n5. THE RESIDENT NOTICE\nA short, plain-language letter sent before any inspection cycle covering the four resident-caused conditions that generate Life-Threatening findings: furniture or storage blocking a door or window, aftermarket double-cylinder deadbolts, window air conditioners in egress windows, and disabled or removed smoke alarms. Give a date by which residents should correct them and an offer of help. It costs a stamp and it removes the most common preventable findings on the property.\n\n6. THE WALK ORDER\nSequence your own pre-inspection walk to find these items first: electrical rooms and panels, mechanical and boiler rooms, then corridors and stairwells for egress and storage, then units — and in each unit, operate every window, check every alarm, open the electrical panel if accessible, and look at the water heater. Everything else can wait for the second pass."}]},{"id":"ch6","unit":"u2","number":6,"title":"Severe & Moderate Deficiencies","subtitle":"The middle of the ladder, where most of a real punch list lives — plus Low, de minimis, and the items deferred from scoring","sections":[{"heading":"How the middle tiers actually behave","body":"Life-Threatening findings get the attention, but Severe and Moderate findings are where most properties actually lose their points, because there are so many more of them.\n\nThe weights:\nSevere    — Unit 40.0, Inside 36.4, Outside 33.1\nModerate  — Unit 20.0, Inside 18.2, Outside 16.5\n\nTwo facts to hold onto:\n\nONE SEVERE IS TWO MODERATES. Exactly. In every area column, Severe is precisely double Moderate. That makes triage arithmetic easy in the field: if you can clear one Severe or two Moderates for the same cost, you are indifferent; anything better than that ratio decides it.\n\nTHE GAP BETWEEN MODERATE AND LOW IS ENORMOUS. Moderate is 20.0 in a unit; Low is 2.4. Moderate is more than eight times Low. This is where most amateur punch lists go wrong — they treat \"Moderate\" and \"Low\" as adjacent, when the scoring model treats them as an order of magnitude apart. If a finding could plausibly be either, it is worth the ten minutes to determine which.\n\nThe two definitions, again, because the boundary between them decides that eight-fold difference:\n\nSEVERE — high risk of PERMANENT DISABILITY, or SERIOUS INJURY OR ILLNESS; or where the resident's PHYSICAL SECURITY OR SAFETY, or that of their property, would be SERIOUSLY COMPROMISED.\n\nMODERATE — MODERATE RISK of an adverse medical event requiring a healthcare visit, causing temporary harm, or, if left untreated, causing or worsening a chronic condition.\n\nThe practical test that resolves most calls: ask whether the pathway to harm is ACUTE or CHRONIC, and whether SECURITY is implicated. Acute serious injury or a security compromise points to Severe. A pathway that runs through repeated exposure, a doctor's visit, or a worsening chronic condition points to Moderate.\n\nCorrection clocks in the scored portfolios: Severe 30 days, Moderate 30 days — the same, which surprises people. The tiers differ in points, not in urgency, once you are below Life-Threatening. In the pass/fail voucher and CPD programs the top two tiers compress: Life-Threatening and Severe generally both carry 24 hours. Read the timeframe off the standard for the program you are working in."},{"heading":"Severe — the security family","body":"This is the family consultants most consistently under-rate, because nobody is going to be injured by a bad lock. Read the definition again: physical security or safety of a resident OR THEIR PROPERTY, seriously compromised. That is a Severe finding on its own terms.\n\nWHAT TYPICALLY LANDS HERE\n• A unit entry door that closes and latches but cannot be locked — broken or missing deadbolt or lockset.\n• A ground-floor or otherwise accessible window that cannot be secured — broken latch, missing lock, sash that will not stay closed.\n• A common-area exterior door that does not latch or lock, leaving the building open.\n• Damaged door frames or strike plates that defeat the lock even though the hardware is present.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 1 — the door that closes but will not lock\nUnit 108's entry door swings, closes and latches. The deadbolt cylinder spins freely and will not throw.\nCall: Severe, Unit area, weight 40.0. Contrast with Chapter 5: a door that will NOT CLOSE OR LATCH AT ALL is a unit entry door finding at the top tier. The distinction is whether the door can perform its function as a door versus whether it can be secured.\nAt a sample of 16, this is 2.50 points for a lockset that costs under fifty dollars installed. It is one of the best points-per-dollar repairs on any property.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 2 — the ground-floor window latch\nA ground-floor bedroom window's latch is broken; the sash closes but cannot be secured.\nCall: Severe. The security pathway is direct at ground level.\nConsultant's note: window latches are the finding most likely to be dismissed by maintenance as trivial. Walk ground-floor and grade-accessible units specifically for them.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 3 — the building entry door\nThe rear common entry door's closer has failed; the door drifts and does not latch, leaving the building accessible.\nCall: Severe, Inside or Outside depending on where the standard places the component — record the location precisely. A door closer is an inexpensive part and a fast repair.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 4 — the distinction that matters\nUnit 210: the deadbolt works but the strike plate is split and the screws pull out of the frame under pressure.\nCall: still Severe. The hardware being present is not the test; whether the assembly actually secures the unit is. This is the case a maintenance supervisor will argue with you about, and you will be right.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 5 — what is NOT in this family\nA resident's chain lock is broken, but the deadbolt and lockset both function.\nCall: not a Severe security finding. The unit can be secured. Note it as a courtesy item if you like; do not score it."},{"heading":"Severe — the injury and illness family","body":"WATER HEATER — TEMPERATURE AND PRESSURE RELIEF\nWater heater TPR deficiencies are treated as Severe health and safety findings, and they have precise criteria:\n• The TPR valve has an active leak, or is obstructed such that it cannot be fully actuated.\n• The relief valve discharge piping is damaged, capped, slopes upward, or is made of unsuitable material.\n• The discharge piping must terminate BETWEEN 2 AND 6 INCHES above the flood level of the waste receptor — a drain pan or floor drain. Termination more than 6 inches or less than 2 inches above the flood level is a defect.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 1 — measuring the discharge\nThree water heaters in three units. Unit A: discharge terminates 4 inches above the floor drain. Unit B: 11 inches. Unit C: 1 inch, resting inside the drain pan.\nCall: A is compliant. B and C are both deficiencies — B is too high, C is too low and risks blockage. Carry a tape measure to every water heater. This is the most measurable finding in the entire program and it is missed constantly because people look at the valve and not the pipe.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 2 — the capped discharge\nA water heater's discharge pipe has a threaded cap installed on the end, apparently to stop dripping.\nCall: capped discharge piping is a listed deficiency. This one is worth explaining to the client in plain terms: the cap converts a relief valve into a pressure vessel.\n\nMOLD-LIKE SUBSTANCE, 1 TO 9 SQUARE FEET\nCovered in detail in Chapters 3 and 5. Cumulative per room: 1 to 9 square feet is Severe.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 3 — the recurring bathroom\nA bathroom shows about 2 square feet of mold-like substance on the ceiling. The exhaust fan runs but moves no air; a tissue held to the grille does not stick.\nCall: two separate findings under two separate standards. The mold at 2 square feet is Severe in the Unit area, 40.0. The ventilation failure is Moderate, 20.0. Combined weight 60.0; at a sample of 12 that is 5.00 points from one bathroom.\nConsultant's note: this pairing is extremely common and it is the clearest example of root cause versus symptom. Cleaning the ceiling without fixing the fan guarantees the finding returns.\n\nFLAMMABLE AND COMBUSTIBLE STORAGE\nCombustible items — hairspray, nail polish remover, butane or charcoal lighter fluid, paint thinner in original containers — stored WITHIN THREE FEET of a heat appliance or fuel-burning water heater is a recorded deficiency, as is storage on or near ignition sources and improperly stored chemicals generally.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 4 — the utility closet\nA unit's water heater closet is being used for household storage, with a can of paint thinner about 18 inches from the burner.\nCall: deficiency. Correction cost: zero. Move the can.\nConsultant's note: this and the boiler-room equivalent are the two findings you can eliminate property-wide with a resident notice and one afternoon of walking. Always on the punch list, never expensive.\n\nSPRINKLER OBSTRUCTION\nAn item or object within 18 inches of a sprinkler head is a deficiency — with exclusions for permanent features of the built environment such as a closet, wall-mounted kitchen cabinets, a permanently installed light fixture, or an exit sign, and for a parked vehicle within 18 inches.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 5 — reading the exclusions\nA storage room has boxes stacked to within 10 inches of a head. A corridor has an exit sign 8 inches from a head. A carport has a parked car 14 inches below a head.\nCall: only the boxes. The exit sign and the parked vehicle are excluded. Knowing the exclusions keeps items off your punch list that were never going to be cited — which is a credibility asset, because clients notice when you make them spend money on nothing."},{"heading":"Moderate — the habitability and chronic-exposure family","body":"Moderate deficiencies are defined by a medical pathway that is real but not acute. In practice this is where most of a working punch list lives.\n\nBATHROOM VENTILATION\nNSPIRE accepts three forms: a mechanical exhaust fan, a passive ventilation system, or an operable window. An operable window must open easily and must not be sealed shut or painted over. Where NONE of the three is present or operable, the bathroom is a Moderate deficiency.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 1 — three bathrooms, three answers\nBathroom A: no fan, no vent, and a window painted shut. Bathroom B: no fan, no vent, and a window that opens freely. Bathroom C: a fan that is present and audibly running but moves no measurable air, and no window.\nCall: A is a deficiency — the painted-shut window does not count as operable. B is compliant. C is a deficiency — present but not operable is not compliant, and the tissue test is how you demonstrate it.\n\nPLUMBING LEAKS\nA plumbing leak is a failure of a plumbing system allowing water intrusion in unintended areas. Inspect all visible connections — traps, supply lines, and direct connections to fixtures — and look for the secondary evidence: mold or mildew, peeling paint or wallpaper, warped or stained walls, buckled or water-stained floors or ceilings.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 2 — the leak you cannot see\nA kitchen has no visible drip, but the cabinet floor under the sink is swollen and delaminating and the base of the cabinet is stained.\nCall: the evidence supports a plumbing leak finding even without an active drip at the moment of inspection. Note also that if mold-like substance is present under the sink, that is a SECOND finding under a separate standard, measured on its own thresholds.\n\nAPPLIANCES AND FIXTURES\nThe affirmative requirements establish that a refrigerator must be present, that hot and cold water must run from sinks, and that a permanently installed light fixture must be present in the kitchen and the bathroom. Beyond presence, the appliances and fixtures a unit does have must function.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 3 — the range\nA four-burner range has one burner that does not ignite; the oven works.\nCall: Moderate. The appliance is present but partially inoperable.\nConsultant's note: this is a fifteen-dollar igniter and a service call in most cases, and at a sample of 12 the Moderate weight of 20.0 is 1.67 points. Good ratio.\n\nFLOORS, WALLS, CEILINGS AND SURFACES\nDamaged floor coverings that create a hazard, damaged wall and ceiling surfaces, and damaged interior doors generally land at Moderate where a genuine hazard or habitability consequence exists.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 4 — carpet versus tile\nA bedroom carpet has a seam separation with a raised edge across the doorway. A hallway has one 4-inch vinyl tile missing, leaving a flush recess in the subfloor.\nCall: the carpet seam is a hazard — it lifts and catches a foot. The missing tile is flush and creates no trip hazard; it is cosmetic and should not be scored, though a client may want it fixed anyway. Note that if the tile recess were deep enough to create a 3/4-inch vertical differential in a walking path, the trip hazard standard would apply.\n\nTRIP HAZARDS\nThe measurable criteria: a 3/4-inch difference in level along the walking path for a vertical hazard, and at least 2 inches of separation for a horizontal hazard.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 5 — measuring the sidewalk\nThree sidewalk locations: a 1/2-inch lift, a 1-inch lift, and a joint that has opened to a 3-inch gap.\nCall: the 1/2-inch lift is under the threshold and is not a deficiency. The 1-inch lift exceeds 3/4 inch and is. The 3-inch gap exceeds the 2-inch horizontal criterion and is.\nConsultant's note: carry a small ruler or a marked block. Sidewalk grinding is inexpensive and this is one of the most reliably correctable Outside findings on any property."},{"heading":"Low, de minimis, and knowing when NOT to cite","body":"Low deficiencies are CRITICAL TO HABITABILITY but carry no substantive health or safety risk. Weights: Unit 2.4, Inside 2.2, Outside 2.0.\n\nDo the arithmetic once and you will never over-invest here again. At a sample of 20, a Low unit deficiency deducts 2.4 divided by 20, which is 0.12 points. A Low outside deficiency deducts 0.10 points. A client could have every Low category on the property in play and lose a couple of points.\n\nThat is the message: LOW ITEMS DO NOT DECIDE OUTCOMES. Never let a client spend meaningful money chasing them while a Severe item sits uncorrected in a sampled unit.\n\nTwo legitimate exceptions:\n1. When a Low item is genuinely free to fix, fix it. There is no reason to leave points on the table when the cost is zero.\n2. When a property is sitting within a point of a cycle boundary — 89.4 needing 90 for the three-year cycle — Low items become the cheapest available fractional points. This is a narrow, arithmetic-driven exception, not a general strategy.\n\nDE MINIMIS AND NOT-RECORDED CONDITIONS\nSome conditions are explicitly below the recording threshold. The clearest is mold-like substance under 4 square inches cumulative in a room. Others come from exclusions written into the standards:\n• Sprinkler clearance obstructed by a permanent built-environment feature — a closet, wall cabinet, permanent light fixture, exit sign — or by a parked vehicle.\n• A fire extinguisher with a missing tag where the owner provides an invoice or report from the servicing company dated within the past year.\n• The elevator certificate: it is verified during the inspection, but neither failure to provide it nor an expired certificate is itself a recorded deficiency.\n• Call-for-aid systems: not required by NSPIRE. If a property has one, it should function, but the absence of a system is not a deficiency.\n\nKnowing these exclusions is worth real money. Half of a consultant's credibility comes from finding things the client missed; the other half comes from telling the client, confidently, that something they were worried about will not be cited.\n\nTHE SIX DEFERRED-SCORING AFFIRMATIVE REQUIREMENTS\nUntil October 1, 2026, six categories are cited but do not deduct points: fire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI outlets or breakers, guardrails, HVAC, interior lighting, and minimum electrical and lighting.\n\nHow to present this honestly, in three sentences a client will remember:\n1. These are inspected and cited today, and they must be corrected on their stated timeframe.\n2. They do not affect the score before October 1, 2026.\n3. On that date the deduction lands in full, all at once, with no phase-in.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO — quantifying the deferred exposure\nA 120-unit property, sample of 24. GFCI protection is absent within 6 feet of water at kitchen and bathroom sinks throughout the property, and habitable rooms in the original floor plan have only one outlet with no permanent light fixture, so the minimum electrical and lighting requirement is unmet across the portfolio.\nToday: cited, corrected, zero points.\nAfter October 1, 2026: the duplicate rule still applies, so within the Unit area these become one scored instance per standard rather than one per unit. Two distinct standards at Unit-area weight, divided by the sample of 24, is a modest number of points — but the CORRECTION cost is a property-wide electrical project, and the correction obligation is live today.\nThe honest framing: the score exposure here is small and the capital exposure is large. Do not oversell the score risk. Sell the fact that a property-wide electrical scope is far cheaper planned over eighteen months than discovered in a 30-day correction window — and that you can scope, sequence and document it."}]},{"id":"ch7","unit":"u3","number":7,"title":"HCV vs. Scored Programs","subtitle":"One standard, two consequences — who gets a score, who gets pass/fail, what the current dates are, and how each changes your service","sections":[{"heading":"The split, stated precisely","body":"NSPIRE is one set of standards. What differs by program is what HUD does with the findings.\n\nSCORED PROGRAMS — Public Housing and project-based Multifamily (PBRA)\nAn inspector working for or contracted by REAC inspects a statistical sample of units plus the Inside and Outside areas. The property receives a 0-100 score. Sixty passes. The Unit Threshold can force a fail on Unit-area deductions alone. The score determines the next inspection interval and feeds HUD asset-management and enforcement decisions.\n\nPASS/FAIL PROGRAMS — Housing Choice Voucher, Project-Based Voucher, Section 8 Mod Rehab, and the CPD programs (ESG, Continuum of Care, HOME, Housing Trust Fund)\nThe inspection covers the individual unit. There is NO numerical score. Deficiencies are recorded and the unit passes or fails. Correction timeframes attach to severity, and failure to correct affects the assistance payment.\n\nThe same physical condition can therefore produce two completely different consequences. A broken entry-door deadbolt at a PBRA property is a Severe finding worth 40.0 of weight, divided by the sample, contributing a fraction of a point to a score. The identical broken deadbolt in a voucher unit produces a failed inspection for that unit with a correction deadline attached and the housing assistance payment on the line.\n\nThat difference should reshape how you sell. To a scored-program owner you sell POINTS. To a voucher landlord you sell CASH FLOW — specifically, avoiding an abated payment.\n\nNote also that in the voucher and CPD programs, Low deficiencies are recorded but generally do not fail the unit and carry no required correction timeframe. That is a meaningful simplification for a landlord's punch list and one you should state plainly, because it lets a small landlord put their limited money where it actually matters."},{"heading":"Where each program stands right now","body":"This is the section to re-verify before every engagement, because HUD has moved these dates repeatedly.\n\nPUBLIC HOUSING and PBRA MULTIFAMILY\nNSPIRE standards effective October 1, 2023. Scored inspections under NSPIRE are the norm today.\n\nTHE AFFIRMATIVE-REQUIREMENT SCORING DATE — October 1, 2026\nJoint Notice PIH 2025-27 / H 2025-05, issued September 30, 2025, postponed the scoring of six affirmative requirements to October 1, 2026: fire-labeled doors; GFCI/AFCI outlet or breaker; guardrail; HVAC; interior lighting; and minimum electrical and lighting. Cited, corrected, not scored until that date.\n\nHCV, PBV and SECTION 8 MOD REHAB — February 1, 2027\nThe compliance date has been extended three times and now sits at February 1, 2027. PIH Notice 2026-18, issued July 15, 2026, carried the current guidance. That notice also announced that HUD DECOMMISSIONED the NSPIRE-V demonstration application, is no longer building an application for PHAs to use, and has paused development of its own software program. PHAs are expected to use their own inspection systems.\n\nThat last point is a genuine business opening and most consultants have not noticed it. A PHA facing a February 2027 compliance date with no HUD-provided software has to select, configure and validate a system, retrain inspectors, and rewrite its administrative plan — and it has to do all of it against standards its staff has not used before.\n\nCPD PROGRAMS — October 1, 2026\nESG, Continuum of Care, HOME and Housing Trust Fund compliance was extended to October 1, 2026. These grantees have had comparatively little program-specific guidance, and many are smaller organizations without dedicated inspection staff.\n\nALWAYS IN EFFECT\nSmoke alarm and carbon monoxide alarm requirements are statutory and were never extended. They apply to every program regardless of transition status.\n\nTHE THREE QUESTIONS\nAt the start of every engagement:\n1. Which program is this property in?\n2. Has that program's NSPIRE compliance date arrived, and if it is a voucher unit, has THIS PHA transitioned yet or is it still running HQS?\n3. Has October 1, 2026 passed, so that the six affirmative requirements are scoring?\nNinety seconds. It prevents the two most common ways of being confidently wrong about a property."},{"heading":"What a fail means for an HCV landlord","body":"For a voucher landlord the inspection is a payment event. Walk a prospective client through the actual sequence and you will usually close the sale on the spot.\n\nTHE SEQUENCE\n1. The PHA inspects the unit and records deficiencies by severity.\n2. Life-Threatening deficiencies — and in the voucher and CPD programs, generally Severe deficiencies as well — must be corrected within 24 hours. Moderate deficiencies generally carry 30 days. Low deficiencies are recorded but generally do not fail the unit and carry no required correction timeframe.\n3. If the owner misses the deadline, the PHA abates the housing assistance payment. Abatement means the subsidy stops. It is not retroactively restored for the abated period.\n4. Under the HOTMA-amended enforcement provisions at 24 CFR 982.404, if the unit still does not comply within 60 days of the noncompliance determination, the housing agency must terminate the HAP contract.\n\nRead step 3 again, because it is the sentence that sells the service. The lost subsidy is gone. A landlord receiving 900 dollars a month in HAP who is abated for two months has lost 1,800 dollars that no later repair recovers. Against that, a pre-inspection walk-through costing a few hundred dollars is obviously worth doing, and you do not have to work hard to make the point.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 1 — the 24-hour failure\nA landlord with four voucher units is inspected on a Thursday afternoon. One unit has a smoke alarm that has been removed from its base by the resident. The landlord is out of town and does not read the notice until Monday.\nOutcome: the 24-hour clock expired over the weekend. Abatement begins.\nPrevention: this is a ten-dollar sealed alarm and a five-minute installation. A pre-inspection walk two weeks earlier would have caught it.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 2 — the resident-caused finding\nA voucher unit fails for furniture blocking the bedroom door and a window AC unit permanently installed in the bedroom's egress window.\nOutcome: both are the OWNER's compliance problem even though the resident created both.\nPrevention: a resident letter before the inspection cycle, plus a walk-through. This is the single most repeatable service you can sell to a small landlord and it costs you an hour.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 3 — the landlord who thinks Low items matter\nA landlord spends a weekend on cosmetic touch-ups and misses a broken entry-door deadbolt.\nOutcome: the deadbolt is a Severe finding on a short clock; the cosmetics were never going to fail the unit.\nYour value: telling a landlord what NOT to do is frequently worth more than the list of what to do.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 4 — the small portfolio\nA landlord owns nine voucher units across three buildings, inspected on a rolling schedule.\nYour offer: an annual walk of all nine against a standardized checklist, a written punch list ranked by severity clock, and a repeat walk of any unit scheduled for inspection within the next sixty days. Flat annual fee. This is the most durable small-client product in the voucher space because the risk recurs every year and the landlord never becomes an expert.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO 5 — the PHA still on HQS\nA landlord's PHA has not transitioned and is still inspecting under HQS in late 2026.\nYour offer: inspect against NSPIRE anyway. Everything NSPIRE requires that HQS did not — sealed or hardwired alarms, CO detection, GFCI near water, minimum outlets and lighting — becomes a planned capital list instead of a February 2027 emergency."},{"heading":"Annual self-inspections — the recurring revenue product","body":"Separate from any HUD-directed inspection, PHAs and owners of HUD housing subject to an assistance contract must ANNUALLY SELF-INSPECT their properties, INCLUDING ALL UNITS, to ensure compliance with the standards. The results must be maintained for THREE YEARS and provided to HUD on request.\n\nRead the three operative facts:\n• ANNUAL. It happens every year, forever. That is a subscription, not a project.\n• ALL UNITS. Not a sample. A 200-unit property means 200 units inspected, every year — which is far more work than the REAC sample and is exactly the kind of volume an owner would rather outsource.\n• RETAINED AND PRODUCIBLE. Records held three years and handed to HUD on request. An owner who cannot produce them has a compliance problem independent of any physical condition.\n\nWhy this is the best product in the NSPIRE consulting space:\n1. It is required, so you are not persuading anyone that they need it.\n2. It recurs annually with no renewal argument.\n3. It is high-volume and process-driven, which means it scales with trained staff rather than with your personal time.\n4. It generates exactly the documentation that supports a technical review request later, which makes your appeals work stronger.\n5. It surfaces systemic conditions — the repeated-by-floor-plan problems that actually determine a REAC score — because you are seeing every unit rather than a sample.\n\nHOW TO STRUCTURE THE DELIVERABLE\n• One record per unit, dated, with the inspector's name.\n• Findings recorded by STANDARD NAME, not by description, with area and severity.\n• Photographs of every finding, timestamped.\n• A property roll-up that separates: items corrected during the visit, items scheduled, and items requiring capital planning.\n• A systemic-conditions section naming any deficiency appearing in more than a stated share of units, with the floor plan or building it tracks to.\n• A retention index so the client can produce any year's records on request without searching.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO — turning a self-inspection into a REAC projection\nYou self-inspect all 96 units of a property. You find a missing GFCI in 71 of them, no bathroom ventilation in the 24 units of one floor plan, and scattered individual issues.\nThe self-inspection deliverable lists all of it. The REAC projection built on top of it says something different and far more useful: within the Unit area the GFCI condition scores once and the ventilation condition scores once, so the score exposure is two Unit-area findings — but the CORRECTION exposure is 95 units of work, and the probability that the REAC sample lands on at least one of each is effectively 100 percent.\nThat is a sentence a client cannot get anywhere else, and it is why the self-inspection and the projection should be sold together."},{"heading":"Inspection frequency, the sample, and scheduling strategy","body":"INSPECTION FREQUENCY IS EARNED\nFor the scored programs, the score sets the interval:\n• 90 or above — inspected at least every three years\n• 80 to 89 — inspected every two years\n• Below 80 — inspected annually\n\nShow a client the six-year arithmetic and the value of moving from 88 to 90 becomes obvious. At a two-year cycle they face three inspection events in six years; at a three-year cycle, two. Each event carries preparation cost, staff disruption, resident notification, and the risk of a bad result. Cutting one event out of every six years is a real, recurring saving — and getting from 88 to 90 is usually a handful of Severe or Moderate items.\n\nFor HCV, inspections occur at least biennially, with a triennial option available to qualifying small rural PHAs.\n\nTHE SAMPLE — WHY EVERY UNIT MATTERS\nThe scoring sample is drawn to reach a 90 percent confidence level with a 6 percent margin of error, capped at 32 units. Neither you nor the owner knows in advance which units will be selected.\n\nThat has one operational consequence and you should say it in exactly these terms: YOU CANNOT PREPARE THE SAMPLE, YOU CAN ONLY PREPARE THE PROPERTY. Managers routinely want to identify \"the good units\" and hope. The arithmetic does not support it — and more importantly, the conditions that actually decide scores are systemic, appearing wherever the sample lands.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO — the systemic condition versus the bad unit\nA 150-unit property has three genuinely bad units and one systemic condition: no GFCI protection anywhere.\nSample of 32. The chance of the sample missing all three bad units is meaningful. The chance of it missing the GFCI condition is zero.\nConclusion: the three bad units are a real but bounded risk; the systemic condition is a certainty. Fix the certainty first. This is the reverse of most managers' instincts, which run toward the visibly worst units.\n\nSCHEDULING STRATEGY, WITHIN THE RULES\nYou cannot choose your inspection date, but you can control readiness. Three legitimate levers:\n1. Time your remediation so corrections are complete and DOCUMENTED before the window opens, not during it.\n2. Time the seasonal findings. Window air conditioners in egress windows appear in summer; heating findings appear in winter. If the property's window falls in a predictable season, address the seasonal conditions in advance.\n3. Time the affirmative requirements. With scoring beginning October 1, 2026, a property whose window falls after that date needs those six categories closed out beforehand; a property inspected before it has additional runway. This is a real, defensible planning conversation and it is the most current thing you can bring to a client right now."}]},{"id":"ch8","unit":"u3","number":8,"title":"Appeals & Building Your Business","subtitle":"The technical review process, what actually wins, the documentation that makes it possible, and how to build the practice around it","sections":[{"heading":"The technical review process","body":"Under NSPIRE, the process for challenging an inspection result is called TECHNICAL REVIEW. The regulations aligned the Public Housing and Multifamily provisions and retired the older UPCS term \"database adjustment\" — using the old term in a client conversation marks you as out of date, so drop it.\n\nHOW IT WORKS\n1. REAC issues a DRAFT inspection report with a draft score and the full record of defects.\n2. The owner or PHA has a defined window to request technical review. Under the NSPIRE provisions for Multifamily, the request must be received by REAC no later than the 45th CALENDAR day following the day HUD provides the inspection report. Calendar days, not business days.\n3. The request is submitted through the NSPIRE system with the evidence supporting the claim. Practice has been to send a copy to NSPIRERegulations@hud.gov with a courtesy copy to the HUD field office representative for the PHA or owner.\n4. REAC reviews and issues a decision. For technical reviews that do not require a new inspection, REAC has indicated a target of 90 days from a complete application, extending to as much as 120 days during the early implementation period.\n5. HUD issues the FINAL inspection report with the final score.\n\nBecause the deadline runs on calendar days from report delivery, the first thing you do when a client engages you post-inspection is calculate the deadline date and put it in writing at the top of your engagement letter. More technical reviews are lost to the calendar than to the merits.\n\nConfirm the current window and submission channel against HUD's published guidance for the applicable program before you file. HUD has revised the administrative procedures more than once, and a filing deadline you learned two years ago is not a fact you should rely on today."},{"heading":"What actually wins — the four grounds","body":"Technical review restores points for VERIFIABLE reasons. The recognized grounds are:\n\n1. HUD OR INSPECTOR ERROR\n2. ADVERSE CONDITIONS BEYOND THE OWNER'S CONTROL\n3. MODERNIZATION WORK IN PROGRESS\n4. CONFLICT WITH STATE OR LOCAL CODE\n\nEverything else is not a ground, no matter how reasonable it sounds. In particular: \"we fixed it immediately,\" \"the resident caused it,\" \"we didn't know,\" and \"we disagree with the correction timeframe\" are not grounds. Learn to say that to a client early, because a client who believes fast repair erases a finding will be angry later.\n\nGROUND 1 — HUD OR INSPECTOR ERROR\nThe strongest and most common winner, and it comes in three shapes:\n• MISCLASSIFIED SEVERITY. The condition was real, but it does not meet the criteria for the tier it was recorded at. This is where measurements win. If mold-like substance was recorded above the 9-square-foot top-tier threshold and your dated photograph with a tape measure in frame shows 6 square feet, you have a specific, checkable, arithmetic argument.\n• CRITERIA NOT MET. The condition does not meet the standard's written criteria at all. A trip hazard recorded at a location where the vertical differential measures 1/2 inch, against a 3/4-inch criterion. A sprinkler obstruction recorded where the obstruction is a permanently installed exit sign, which the standard excludes. A fire extinguisher tag deficiency where a servicing company's invoice dated within the past year exists. An elevator certificate cited as a deficiency at all, when the standard provides that neither its absence nor its expiration is itself recorded.\n• WRONG LOCATION OR AREA. A deficiency attributed to the Unit area that was observed in a common area, or attributed to a unit that was not in the sample. This matters disproportionately because of the Unit Threshold — moving one Severe finding out of the Unit column can be the difference between a 59 and a real score.\n\nGROUND 2 — ADVERSE CONDITIONS BEYOND THE OWNER'S CONTROL\nDocumented events outside the owner's control that produced the condition. This ground requires contemporaneous evidence: dated incident reports, weather records, insurance claims, police reports, utility outage records. It is not a general hardship argument.\n\nGROUND 3 — MODERNIZATION WORK IN PROGRESS\nDeficiencies in areas under active modernization at the time of inspection. Requires the contract, the schedule, permits, and evidence that the work was genuinely underway on the inspection date — not merely planned or funded.\n\nGROUND 4 — CONFLICT WITH STATE OR LOCAL CODE\nThe cited condition complies with, or is required by, an applicable state or local code that conflicts with the NSPIRE criterion. Requires the code citation and, ideally, a letter from the local authority having jurisdiction. This ground comes up most often in elevator, boiler, sprinkler and fire-alarm work, where local jurisdictions impose their own inspection intervals, permitting and certificate regimes.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO — a filing that wins\nA 60-unit property receives a draft score of 58, a fail. Reviewing the report you find: (a) a Severe mold finding recorded at 11 square feet, which your dated pre-inspection photograph with a tape measure shows at about 5 square feet in the same room; (b) a trip hazard on a walkway where you measure 5/8 inch; and (c) a Unit-area exposed-conductor finding attributed to unit 214, which the report's own sample list does not include.\nThe filing addresses each with a specific ground, a specific piece of evidence, and a specific point calculation showing the effect on the score. It does not argue about fairness, does not mention how fast anything was fixed, and does not bundle unrelated complaints. That is what a winnable filing looks like."},{"heading":"The documentation system that makes appeals possible","body":"You cannot build an appeal after the fact. Either the evidence exists from before the inspection or it does not. Everything in this section is work you sell BEFORE anything goes wrong.\n\nTHE PRE-INSPECTION EVIDENCE FILE\nFor every finding you identify during a pre-inspection:\n• The STANDARD NAME as HUD publishes it.\n• The precise LOCATION — building, floor, unit number, room, and where in the room.\n• The MEASUREMENT where the standard has one, with a tape measure or ruler IN THE FRAME of the photograph. This single habit wins more technical reviews than anything else you can do.\n• A dated, timestamped photograph. Confirm the device clock is correct; a wrong timestamp destroys the evidentiary value of the whole set.\n• The severity you assigned and the reasoning in one sentence.\n\nTHE CORRECTION RECORD\nFor every item corrected:\n• Before photograph, after photograph, both timestamped.\n• Date and time of correction, and who performed it.\n• Invoice, work order or purchase receipt.\n• For interim repairs, note that it is interim and record the scheduled date of the full repair — an interim measure such as a blank cover plate over a missing receptacle must still be followed by a full repair within a reasonable timeframe approved by HUD or its designee.\n\nTHE CERTIFICATE AND SERVICE FILE\nKept current and indexed, because inspectors will ask for these on site and images of certificates provided to the inspector appear in the report:\n• Fire extinguisher service records — the servicing company's invoice or report dated within the past year is the item that defeats a missing-tag citation.\n• Sprinkler and fire alarm inspection, testing and maintenance records.\n• Boiler inspection certificates as required by state or local jurisdiction.\n• Elevator inspection certificate — verify it and keep it current for local compliance, while understanding that under NSPIRE its absence or expiration is not itself a recorded deficiency.\n• Pest control service records, which support both the condition history and a beyond-owner-control argument.\n\nTHE SELF-INSPECTION ARCHIVE\nThe annual self-inspections, retained three years as required. Beyond satisfying the requirement, this archive is what lets you demonstrate a condition's history — that it did not exist at last year's walk, or that it was identified and remediated on a documented schedule.\n\nWHY THIS IS A PRODUCT AND NOT AN OVERHEAD\nMost clients will not pay for documentation in the abstract. They will pay for it framed three ways:\n1. \"This is what a technical review runs on, and the window is 45 calendar days — there is no time to build a file after the report arrives.\"\n2. \"This is what you hand an inspector on site so they do not have to guess.\"\n3. \"This is what proves you met your annual self-inspection obligation when HUD asks for the records.\"\nSame file, three justifications, and at least one of them lands with any client."},{"heading":"The service catalogue and how to price it","body":"Five products, in the order a client will normally buy them.\n\n1. THE BASELINE ASSESSMENT\nA full walk of Inside and Outside areas plus a unit sample larger than REAC's. Deliverable: a projected score with a stated range and assumptions, the Unit Threshold test as a separate headline number, and a punch list ranked by points recovered per dollar. This is the entry product and the one that demonstrates your competence fastest.\nPricing logic: per-property base plus a per-unit rate. Price the range so the projection is the deliverable, not the walk.\n\n2. THE ANNUAL SELF-INSPECTION\nAll units, every year, documented and indexed to satisfy the three-year retention requirement. This is the subscription. See Chapter 7.\nPricing logic: per-unit annual rate, discounted at volume, billed on a schedule rather than per visit.\n\n3. REMEDIATION MANAGEMENT\nSequencing the punch list, coordinating vendors, verifying each correction against the WRITTEN CRITERION rather than against appearance, and building the correction record as the work proceeds.\nPricing logic: a percentage of the managed repair cost, or a flat monthly management fee. Either way, align your incentive with the client's — you are paid for points recovered and money not wasted, not for volume of work ordered.\n\n4. TECHNICAL REVIEW SUPPORT\nReviewing the draft report line by line against the standards, identifying the defensible grounds, assembling the evidence, calculating the point effect of each challenged item, and drafting the submission.\nPricing logic: fixed fee for the review and drafting. Avoid pure contingency — it creates pressure to file weak grounds, and a pattern of weak filings damages your standing with the same field office you will be in front of again.\n\n5. THE 24-HOUR PROTOCOL AND STAFF TRAINING\nBuilding the vendor card, spending-authority letter, pre-stocked inventory list, documentation template and resident notice from Chapter 5, then training site staff to use them.\nPricing logic: one-time setup fee plus an annual refresh. Low effort, high perceived value, and it embeds you in the client's operations.\n\nTHE PACKAGING THAT WORKS\nSell 1 as a standalone first engagement. Convert to 2 plus 5 as an annual retainer. Bill 3 as it arises. Hold 4 in reserve — and if the client never needs it, say so out loud, because a consultant who tells a client they do not need a service is the one who gets the next referral."},{"heading":"Getting the first client and scaling past yourself","body":"THE FIRST CLIENT\nThe fastest route in is not marketing. It is a specific, current, checkable fact that the prospect does not know. Right now you have three of them:\n\n• \"Six requirement categories — fire-labeled doors, GFCI, guardrails, HVAC, interior lighting, and minimum electrical and lighting — start deducting points on October 1, 2026. They are being cited today with no score effect. If your inspection window falls after that date, the exposure you are carrying right now becomes visible all at once.\"\n• \"The voucher compliance date moved to February 1, 2027, and HUD decommissioned the NSPIRE-V demonstration application and paused development of its own software. If you administer vouchers, you are selecting and validating a system on your own timeline.\"\n• \"Your annual self-inspection covers every unit, not a sample, and the records have to be retained three years and produced on request. When did you last do one, and can you produce the file?\"\n\nEach of these is verifiable, current, and lands as expertise rather than as a pitch. Lead with the one that fits the prospect's program.\n\nTHE FIRST ENGAGEMENT\nUnderprice it deliberately and overdeliver on the DOCUMENTATION. Your goal is not the fee; it is a case study with real numbers: starting projected score, items identified by severity, cost of remediation, result. One documented case study with specific numbers opens more doors than any brochure, and you cannot buy one.\n\nTwo rules for the first engagement:\n• Say what you do not know. If you cannot verify a current standard, tell the client you are flagging it rather than guessing. Clients remember that, and it is also the only defensible way to work in a program whose standards are versioned and revised.\n• Never promise a score. Promise a methodology, a documented punch list, and a range. Consultants who guarantee scores are one bad sample from losing the account.\n\nSCALING\nThe constraint is your own time in units. Three levers, in order:\n1. STANDARDIZE THE FIELD PROCESS. One checklist keyed to standard names, one photo protocol, one report template. Until this exists you cannot delegate anything.\n2. HIRE FOR THE VOLUME WORK. Annual self-inspections are process work. A trained inspector following your checklist can do them; your judgment is needed for the projection, the prioritization and the technical review.\n3. SPECIALIZE BY PROGRAM OR ASSET TYPE. Small voucher landlords, PHAs facing the February 2027 date, CPD grantees facing October 1, 2026, or scored PBRA portfolios are four different businesses with different buying cycles. Pick the one where your existing relationships are and go deep before going wide.\n\nMAINTAINING COMPETENCE\nThe standards are versioned and HUD revises them. Build two habits and you will stay ahead of most of the market: re-check the published standards and notices before every engagement, and write the version or revision date at the top of your own reference document so a client can see how current your work is. A consultant working from a two-year-old memory of the standards is a liability to their client and, eventually, to themselves."}]}];
export const CHAPTER_QUIZZES = {"ch1":{"title":"Chapter 1 Quiz — What Is NSPIRE?","questions":[{"q":"A maintenance supervisor tells you a hallway has 'a really bad railing situation.' What is your first question?","opts":["How much will it cost to replace?","Which standard does it fall under, and which listed deficiency does the condition match?","How long has it been like that?","Has REAC cited it before?"],"ans":1,"exp":"Severity is a property of the written deficiency, not an opinion. Identify the standard and the specific deficiency bullet, and the severity and correction timeframe come with it."},{"q":"NSPIRE replaced which two systems?","opts":["UPCS and REAC","HQS and MOR","UPCS and HQS","PASS and NSPIRE-V"],"ans":2,"exp":"NSPIRE merged UPCS (scored, sampled, Public Housing and Multifamily) and HQS (pass/fail, vouchers) into one standard."},{"q":"A 40-unit PBRA property has nothing broken in a bedroom, but the room has only one working outlet and no permanently installed light fixture. Under NSPIRE, is this citable?","opts":["No — nothing is broken or damaged","Yes — the minimum electrical and lighting affirmative requirement is not met","Only if the resident complains","Only in Public Housing, not PBRA"],"ans":1,"exp":"Affirmative requirements are things that must be PRESENT. A habitable room needs two working outlets, or one outlet plus a permanent light fixture. Nothing has to be broken for the requirement to be unmet."},{"q":"Which is the correct current compliance date for HCV, PBV and Section 8 Mod Rehab?","opts":["October 1, 2023","October 1, 2025","February 1, 2027","There is no date; vouchers are permanently exempt"],"ans":2,"exp":"The voucher compliance date has been extended three times and now sits at February 1, 2027, carried in PIH Notice 2026-18 (July 15, 2026)."},{"q":"Which six requirement categories are cited but NOT scored until October 1, 2026?","opts":["Smoke alarms, CO alarms, egress, mold, infestation, and sharp edges","Fire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI, guardrails, HVAC, interior lighting, and minimum electrical and lighting","Elevators, boilers, sprinklers, fire alarms, chimneys, and water heaters","Roofs, foundations, walkways, fencing, site lighting, and playgrounds"],"ans":1,"exp":"Joint Notice PIH 2025-27 / H 2025-05 postponed scoring of these six affirmative requirements to October 1, 2026. They are still inspected, cited, and required to be corrected."},{"q":"A client says, 'The affirmative requirements aren't scored, so we're skipping them this year.' The best correction is:","opts":["Agree — there is no score impact, so there is no reason to spend money","Explain that they are still cited and still require correction, and that the deduction arrives all at once on October 1, 2026","Tell them to appeal any citation of an unscored item","Tell them the requirement has been repealed"],"ans":1,"exp":"Unscored is not unregulated. The items are cited and must be corrected, and a property with widespread GFCI or lighting gaps carries a latent deduction that lands in full when scoring begins."},{"q":"Which statement about smoke and carbon monoxide alarm requirements is correct?","opts":["They were postponed with the other affirmative requirements to October 1, 2026","They apply only to Public Housing","They are statutory and remain in effect regardless of program or any extension","They apply only after a PHA transitions from HQS"],"ans":2,"exp":"Smoke and CO alarm requirements are statutory in nature and were never part of any NSPIRE extension."},{"q":"Which of these best describes an 'affirmative requirement'?","opts":["A deficiency that must be corrected within 24 hours","A condition that must be PRESENT, so its absence is citable even when nothing is broken","A requirement affirmed by the owner in writing","A deficiency that HUD scores at double weight"],"ans":1,"exp":"Affirmative requirements set a floor for what must exist — outlets, lighting, GFCI protection — rather than only prohibiting defects."},{"q":"A PHA tells you it is still inspecting voucher units under HQS in late 2026. Is that permissible?","opts":["No — NSPIRE has been mandatory for all programs since October 2023","Yes — the HCV compliance date is February 1, 2027, so a PHA that has not yet transitioned may still be on HQS","Only with a written waiver from REAC","Only for units built before 1978"],"ans":1,"exp":"Until the February 1, 2027 compliance date, a PHA that has not transitioned may still be conducting voucher inspections under HQS. Always confirm which standard the specific PHA is using."},{"q":"What is the annual self-inspection requirement?","opts":["PHAs and owners must self-inspect a 10 percent sample each year","PHAs and owners under an assistance contract must annually self-inspect the property including all units, retain the results, and produce them to HUD on request","Self-inspection is optional and only recommended before a REAC visit","Self-inspection replaces the REAC inspection for properties scoring above 90"],"ans":1,"exp":"The self-inspection covers all units, not a sample, is required annually, and the records must be retained and produced on request."},{"q":"Which of these did NSPIRE NOT change?","opts":["The passing score of 60 on a 0-100 scale","The relative weighting of in-unit conditions","The addition of affirmative requirements","The use of measurable numeric criteria in place of judgment language"],"ans":0,"exp":"The 0-100 scale and the 60 passing threshold carried over. The weighting, the affirmative requirements, and the measurable criteria are the changes."},{"q":"For a scored inspection, what is the sequence of reports?","opts":["A single final report issued on site","A draft report with a draft score and full defect record, then a final report with a final score after the technical review window","A final report first, then a draft if the owner objects","Only a Health and Safety Report is issued"],"ans":1,"exp":"REAC issues the draft report and draft score first. The final report and final score follow the technical review process — which is why the appeals work happens in that gap."},{"q":"Which is the strongest reason NSPIRE's measurable criteria matter to a pre-inspection consultant?","opts":["They make the standards shorter","They let you predict what an inspector will write down, using a tape measure rather than an opinion","They reduce the number of standards","They eliminate the need to read the standards"],"ans":1,"exp":"Prediction is the product. Numeric criteria — 3/4 inch, 18 inches, 4 square inches, 6 feet — are what make prediction possible."},{"q":"A client asks you to guarantee their property will score at least 75. The right response is:","opts":["Agree, and price the engagement to cover the risk","Decline to guarantee a score, and instead commit to a defensible methodology, a documented punch list, and a projected range","Agree only if you also manage the repairs","Agree only for properties under 20 units"],"ans":1,"exp":"You do not control the sample and cannot guarantee an outcome. Selling methodology and documentation is both honest and more durable as a business."},{"q":"Which of the four revenue services is typically hardest for the client's own maintenance staff to replicate?","opts":["Prediction of the score","Prioritization — ranking a punch list by points recovered per dollar","Remediation management","Documentation for appeals"],"ans":1,"exp":"Maintenance staff can fix things and often know what is broken. Ranking work by score impact requires the Defect Impact Weights, the sample-size divisor, and the threshold rules — which is the consultant's specific edge."}]},"ch2":{"title":"Chapter 2 Quiz — The 3 Inspectable Areas","questions":[{"q":"A missing outlet cover plate is found in a ground-floor community room. What is the inspectable area?","opts":["Unit","Inside","Outside","Not inspectable"],"ans":1,"exp":"A community room is common area within the building envelope, so it is Inside."},{"q":"A resident's attached second-floor balcony has a rotted deck board. Area?","opts":["Unit — only that household uses it","Inside","Outside — attached balconies are building exterior components","It depends on whether the balcony is enclosed"],"ans":2,"exp":"Attached porches, patios, balconies and carports are listed Outside components regardless of how many households use them."},{"q":"Why does the inspectable area matter more than most people assume?","opts":["Because each area is inspected by a different inspector","Because the area sets the point weight AND because Unit deductions are tested separately against the 30-point Unit Threshold","Because only Unit deficiencies require correction","Because Outside deficiencies are never cited"],"ans":1,"exp":"Area determines the Defect Impact Weight and determines whether the deduction counts toward the Unit Threshold, which can fail a property on its own."},{"q":"Rank the point weight of the same severity deficiency across areas, highest first.","opts":["Outside, Inside, Unit","Inside, Unit, Outside","Unit, Inside, Outside","All three are weighted identically"],"ans":2,"exp":"Unit carries the highest weight at every severity level, then Inside, then Outside — by design, to focus attention where residents live."},{"q":"A boiler room has paint thinner stored two feet from the fuel-burning water heater. Area, and why does it matter to your walk sequence?","opts":["Unit — the water heater serves units","Inside — and mechanical rooms should be walked first because findings there are fast, cheap, and entirely under management's control","Outside — mechanical equipment is a building system","Inside — but it should be walked last because it rarely produces findings"],"ans":1,"exp":"Boiler and mechanical rooms are Inside. Combustibles within three feet of a heat appliance or fuel-burning water heater is a deficiency, and these rooms produce a high yield of correctable findings per hour."},{"q":"A client wants to spend the entire pre-inspection budget on landscaping, sign repainting and sealcoating. The most accurate response is:","opts":["Good plan — Outside is 15 percent of the score","Those are Outside items with the lowest weight per deficiency, and most of them are not deficiencies at all; the budget will buy almost no points","Good plan — inspectors score curb appeal separately","Those items are Inside, not Outside"],"ans":1,"exp":"Cosmetic site work is the classic misallocation. Outside carries the lowest weight, and general tidiness is not a scored deficiency."},{"q":"Five identical fire-door findings are observed in Inside common areas. For scoring purposes:","opts":["All five deduct separately","Repeat deficiencies within the same inspectable area are counted once","Only the first three count","They are averaged"],"ans":1,"exp":"The duplicate rule counts a repeated deficiency once within the same inspectable area — a rule amateur projections routinely get wrong in both directions."},{"q":"A failing roof has produced water stains and mold-like substance in four units. How should this be presented to the client?","opts":["As four separate Unit repairs","As one Outside repair that is the root of several highest-weighted Unit deductions","As an Inside finding because the water travels through the building","As a cosmetic issue"],"ans":1,"exp":"Envelope failures are where Outside genuinely moves a score — one roof repair retires a cluster of Unit-area mold and water findings."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an Outside component?","opts":["A detached garage","A retaining wall","An interior stairwell handrail","A project sign"],"ans":2,"exp":"Interior stairwells are Inside. Detached garages, retaining walls and project signs are listed Outside components."},{"q":"Furniture belonging to the resident is blocking the bedroom door of a sampled unit. What is the correct call?","opts":["Not a deficiency — it is resident property","A deficiency — blocked egress is cited even when the obstruction is resident-owned","A deficiency only if the resident refuses to move it","An advisory note only"],"ans":1,"exp":"Blocked egress is cited regardless of who owns the obstruction, which is why a pre-inspection resident notice and walk-through is such cheap insurance."},{"q":"A permanently installed window air conditioner sits in the only egress window of a bedroom. The correct classification is:","opts":["Unit, blocked egress, Life-Threatening","Inside, HVAC, Moderate","Outside, building exterior, Low","Not citable — window units are permitted"],"ans":0,"exp":"The unit interior is the area, the standard is egress, and blocked egress is Life-Threatening with a 24-hour clock."},{"q":"A water heater sits in a hall closet serving one dwelling. A functionally identical water heater sits in the building's mechanical room. What differs?","opts":["Nothing — same equipment, same treatment","The area: the first is a Unit finding, the second is Inside — which changes the point weight and whether it counts toward the Unit Threshold","Only the correction timeframe","The second is not inspectable"],"ans":1,"exp":"Same equipment class, different area, different weight, and only the in-unit one feeds the Unit Threshold test."},{"q":"Which sequencing advice for a pre-inspection walk is best supported by how the areas work?","opts":["Start with the units, since they carry the most weight","Start with Inside common areas and mechanical spaces, then units, then the site","Start with the site to build rapport","Order does not matter"],"ans":1,"exp":"Inside findings are fast, cheap, need no resident scheduling, and recur at nearly every property — the highest yield per hour early in the engagement."},{"q":"A sinkhole has opened in a walkway. Which statement is correct?","opts":["It cannot be Life-Threatening because Outside is the lowest-weighted area","Area weight and severity are independent — an Outside finding can absolutely be Life-Threatening and carry a 24-hour clock","Outside deficiencies have no correction timeframe","It would be scored as a Unit deficiency because residents walk on it"],"ans":1,"exp":"Weight and severity are separate coordinates. Low weight does not mean low severity, and the correction clock follows the severity."},{"q":"What is the single most reliable field habit for getting area assignments right?","opts":["Assign areas at the end of the day from photographs","Record the area on the same line as the deficiency at the moment of observation","Assign everything to Unit and correct later","Ask the maintenance supervisor"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reconstructing area assignments after the fact from photos is how projections drift away from the real report."}]},"ch3":{"title":"Chapter 3 Quiz — The 4 Severity Levels","questions":[{"q":"A ground-floor unit's entry door deadbolt is broken and the door cannot be locked. Severity, and why?","opts":["Low — nothing is physically dangerous","Moderate — it may cause stress","Severe — the Severe definition covers deficiencies that seriously compromise a resident's physical security or safety","Not citable — locks are the resident's responsibility"],"ans":2,"exp":"The Severe definition explicitly includes serious compromise of physical security or safety, not just injury risk. This is the most commonly under-rated category."},{"q":"Cumulative mold-like patches in one room measure about 12 square feet. Severity?","opts":["Moderate","Severe","Life-Threatening","Below de minimis"],"ans":2,"exp":"More than 9 square feet cumulative in a room is the top tier — 24-hour clock."},{"q":"Cumulative mold-like patches in one room measure about 3 square feet. Severity?","opts":["Moderate","Severe","Life-Threatening","Below de minimis"],"ans":1,"exp":"The 1-to-9-square-foot band is Severe."},{"q":"A patch of mold-like substance measures 3 square inches. What do you record?","opts":["Moderate","Low","Nothing — it is below the 4-square-inch de minimis floor","Life-Threatening if in a bathroom"],"ans":2,"exp":"Under 4 square inches cumulative in a room is below de minimis and is not recorded. Mention the moisture source to the client, but do not score it."},{"q":"HUD's definition of a Moderate deficiency turns on:","opts":["Cost of repair","Moderate risk of an adverse medical event requiring a healthcare visit, temporary harm, or worsening a chronic condition if untreated","Whether the resident complained","Whether it is visible from the corridor"],"ans":1,"exp":"Moderate is defined by a medical pathway, which is why absent bathroom ventilation qualifies and a crooked cabinet door does not."},{"q":"'Low' deficiencies are best described as:","opts":["Anything cosmetic","Critical to habitability but without substantive health or safety risk","Deficiencies under $100 to repair","Deficiencies found only outside"],"ans":1,"exp":"The phrase 'critical to habitability' is doing real work — purely cosmetic issues with no habitability consequence are generally below the recording threshold entirely."},{"q":"In the scored Public Housing and Multifamily portfolios, the general correction timeframes are:","opts":["LT 24 hours, Severe 30 days, Moderate 30 days, Low 60 days","All deficiencies 24 hours","LT 48 hours, everything else 90 days","LT 24 hours, everything else 14 days"],"ans":0,"exp":"That is the general pattern — but the authoritative timeframe is printed on each standard, per program, and a handful of standards deviate."},{"q":"Where should you read the definitive correction timeframe for a specific deficiency?","opts":["From the severity tier alone","From the standard document itself, which carries the timeframe per program","From the inspector on site","From the prior year's report"],"ans":1,"exp":"Severity implies the default, but each published standard carries its own timeframe by program column. Working from a memorized table eventually burns you."},{"q":"Infestation evidence is found in six sampled units and the laundry room. Severity?","opts":["Moderate — one finding per location","Life-Threatening — infestation across multiple units and common areas escalates to the top tier with a 24-hour clock","Low","Not citable without live insects"],"ans":1,"exp":"Extent drives the escalation. Note also that droppings, shed skins and egg cases are sufficient evidence — live insects are not required."},{"q":"Roughly how large is a cockroach egg case, per the standard's evidence description?","opts":["1 to 2 mm","5 to 9 mm","2 to 3 cm","Size is not specified"],"ans":1,"exp":"Brown oblong cases roughly 5 to 9 mm long are listed among the evidence markers, along with shed skins and droppings."},{"q":"A stored box sits 12 inches from a sprinkler head in a storage room; elsewhere, a permanently installed exit sign sits 10 inches from another head. Which is a deficiency?","opts":["Both","Neither","The box only — permanent built-environment features such as exit signs, cabinets and light fixtures are excluded from the 18-inch obstruction rule","The exit sign only"],"ans":2,"exp":"The 18-inch rule carves out permanent features of the built environment, and a parked vehicle within 18 inches is likewise not the deficiency."},{"q":"An owner corrects a Life-Threatening deficiency within one hour of the inspector leaving. What happens to the deduction?","opts":["It is removed automatically","It remains scored; correction discharges the safety obligation but does not un-write the observed finding","It is halved","It converts to a Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"The only route to changing the final report is the technical review process, and speed of repair is not one of its grounds."},{"q":"Which is NOT a listed ground for technical review?","opts":["HUD or inspector error","Adverse conditions beyond the owner's control","Modernization work in progress","The owner corrected the item quickly"],"ans":3,"exp":"Fast correction is good practice but is not a ground for restoring points. Error, conditions beyond control, modernization in progress, and conflict with state or local code are."},{"q":"A 400-unit property gets one Life-Threatening finding in one sampled unit. A client concludes 'it barely moved our score, so it didn't matter.' The correction is:","opts":["They are right — score impact is the only consequence","The 24-hour correction clock, the documentation obligation and the enforcement exposure are unaffected by how the arithmetic divides out","The finding will be removed at the final report","The Unit Threshold automatically fails them"],"ans":1,"exp":"Score and safety obligations are separate. The Health and Safety Report drives correction duties independent of the numeric result."},{"q":"What is the highest-value habit when a standard has a measurable escalation rule?","opts":["Estimate visually and move on","Measure it, photograph the measurement, and write the number in the report","Always assume the higher tier","Always assume the lower tier"],"ans":1,"exp":"Escalation rules are decided by numbers. A recorded measurement is what makes your projection defensible and your technical review request credible."}]},"ch4":{"title":"Chapter 4 Quiz — Scoring Mechanics","questions":[{"q":"State the NSPIRE scoring formula.","opts":["100 minus the number of deficiencies","100 minus the sum of (Defect Impact Weight x count) divided by the number of units in the inspected sample","100 minus (total deficiencies divided by total units at the property)","The average of the three area scores"],"ans":1,"exp":"Weight, count, and the sample-size divisor. The divisor is the sample, not the property."},{"q":"One Life-Threatening unit deficiency at a property with a sample of 8. Points deducted?","opts":["60.0","7.50","3.00","1.88"],"ans":1,"exp":"60.0 divided by 8 equals 7.50 points."},{"q":"The same Life-Threatening unit deficiency at a property with a sample of 32. Points deducted?","opts":["7.50","3.00","1.88","60.0"],"ans":2,"exp":"60.0 divided by 32 equals 1.88. Same condition, same 24-hour clock, one quarter the score impact."},{"q":"What is the maximum number of units in a NSPIRE scoring sample?","opts":["20","27","32","There is no cap"],"ans":2,"exp":"NSPIRE raised the maximum scoring sample to 32 units, up from 27 under UPCS. The sample targets 90 percent confidence with a 6 percent margin of error."},{"q":"Unit-area deductions total 34.0 points and the raw score is 66. What is the outcome?","opts":["Pass at 66","Pass at 60","The Unit Threshold is triggered and the score is adjusted to 59 — a failure","The property is rescored without the unit findings"],"ans":2,"exp":"Thirty or more points of Unit-area deduction adjusts the score to 59 regardless of how good the raw score looks."},{"q":"A property fails both thresholds. Why does that change the remediation plan?","opts":["It does not — points are points","Clearing the Property Threshold can use cheap Inside and Outside work, but clearing the Unit Threshold requires reducing Unit-area deductions specifically","The Unit Threshold clears automatically once the score passes 60","Only Life-Threatening items count toward the Unit Threshold"],"ans":1,"exp":"Inside and Outside repairs do nothing for the Unit Threshold. A plan that ignores this spends money and still fails."},{"q":"The same deficiency under the same standard is found in six sampled units. For scoring:","opts":["Six deductions","One deduction for the Unit area","Three deductions","It is averaged across the sample"],"ans":1,"exp":"Repeat deficiencies within the same inspectable area score once — but all six instances still carry their own correction obligation."},{"q":"The same deficiency is found in three units AND two corridors. For scoring:","opts":["One deduction total","Two deductions — one for the Unit area and one for the Inside area, at different weights","Five deductions","Two deductions at identical weights"],"ans":1,"exp":"The duplicate rule collapses within an area, not across areas, and Unit and Inside carry different weights. Only the Unit instance feeds the Unit Threshold."},{"q":"A client fixes 19 of 20 instances of the same Severe unit deficiency. Score effect?","opts":["Nineteen twentieths of the deduction is removed","None — the deficiency still exists in the Unit area, so the single scored instance remains","The severity drops to Moderate","The deduction is halved"],"ans":1,"exp":"Partial remediation of a repeated deficiency buys zero points. It still matters enormously for safety and liability, but not for the score."},{"q":"Within the Unit column, how many Low deficiencies equal one Life-Threatening deficiency?","opts":["Five","Ten","Twenty-five","One hundred"],"ans":2,"exp":"60.0 divided by 2.4 is 25. The severity spread dwarfs the area spread, which is why you work the severity ladder rather than the map."},{"q":"Roughly how much less does an Outside deficiency deduct than the same-severity Unit deficiency?","opts":["About 17 percent less","About 50 percent less","About 75 percent less","About 95 percent less"],"ans":0,"exp":"Outside runs about 83 percent of the Unit weight at every severity. Outside is cheap because the repairs are cheap, not because the weights are trivial."},{"q":"Why are large properties NOT protected by their larger sample?","opts":["They are — individual findings always cost less","Because systemic conditions repeat across floor plans and buildings, so they appear wherever the sample lands","Because HUD inspects all units above 200","Because the cap is removed above 200 units"],"ans":1,"exp":"The statistical cushion protects against isolated bad units, not against a repeated condition. Large-property risk is concentrated in the worst REPEATED condition."},{"q":"You have no prior report and cannot confirm the sample size. Best practice?","opts":["Assume 20","Use a third-party sample-size table","Run the projection at two or three plausible sample sizes and give the client a range","Decline the engagement"],"ans":2,"exp":"A range with stated assumptions is more honest and more useful than false precision. When a prior report exists, read the sample size off it."},{"q":"In worked example 6, the 24-unit property scored 52.2. Where did most of the damage come from?","opts":["The units","Inside and Outside combined, which contributed more than half the deduction from cheap, management-controlled conditions","A single Life-Threatening finding","The Unit Threshold"],"ans":1,"exp":"Unit deductions were 22.47 of 47.80. The open panel port, expired extinguisher tag, boiler-room combustibles and site items were where the recoverable points were."},{"q":"Before using the Defect Impact Weight table in a client deliverable you should:","opts":["Nothing — the table is fixed permanently","Confirm it against HUD's published scoring notice for the applicable inspection year","Ask the inspector for their copy","Round all values to the nearest whole number"],"ans":1,"exp":"HUD has revised scoring guidance more than once. Verifying the current table is part of the professional standard of care."}]},"ch5":{"title":"Chapter 5 Quiz — Life-Threatening Deficiencies","questions":[{"q":"HUD defines Life-Threatening deficiencies as those presenting:","opts":["Any risk of injury","A high risk of death to a resident","A high risk of permanent disability","A moderate risk requiring a healthcare visit"],"ans":1,"exp":"High risk of DEATH is the top tier. High risk of permanent disability or serious injury is the Severe definition."},{"q":"Under the exposed-conductor standard, what opening size is the key measurable criterion?","opts":["Greater than 1/4 inch","Greater than 1/2 inch","Greater than 1 inch","Greater than 2 inches"],"ans":1,"exp":"An opening or gap greater than 1/2 inch that leaves a conductor unenclosed is the criterion — carry a half-inch reference and measure the borderline cases."},{"q":"An outlet cover plate is present but has a hairline crack. No conductor is visible. Correct call?","opts":["Cite it as an exposed conductor","Do not cite it; recommend replacement anyway since plates are trivially cheap","Cite it as Severe","Cite it as Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"No opening approaching 1/2 inch and no exposed conductor means no deficiency. Being able to decline to cite is as valuable as being able to cite."},{"q":"A working smoke alarm in a 1998 building is powered by a user-replaceable 9-volt battery. Is it compliant?","opts":["Yes — it works when tested","No — alarms must be hardwired or have a sealed tamper-resistant 10-year battery","Yes, if the battery was replaced this year","Only in Public Housing"],"ans":1,"exp":"The power source is part of the requirement. The device working on the test button does not satisfy it."},{"q":"For units built or substantially rehabilitated after December 2022, smoke alarms must be:","opts":["Sealed 10-year battery or hardwired","Hardwired only","Battery only","Interconnected wirelessly"],"ans":1,"exp":"Post-December-2022 construction and substantial rehab must be hardwired; the sealed-battery alternative is not available."},{"q":"A resident installed a deadbolt requiring a key from the inside and keeps the key on a nearby hook. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant, because the key is accessible","Blocked egress — locks requiring a key, tool or special knowledge from the egress side are prohibited","Moderate deficiency","Not citable because the resident installed it"],"ans":1,"exp":"The standard prohibits the lock type regardless of where the key is kept, and resident installation does not exempt the owner."},{"q":"Unit 405 has a permanently installed window AC in the bedroom's only egress window. Unit 407 has a portable AC hosed through a window that still opens fully. Which is cited?","opts":["Both","Neither","405 only","407 only"],"ans":2,"exp":"The test is whether the window can still function as a means of egress. A permanently installed unit blocking it is cited; a hose through an operable window is not."},{"q":"A bathroom has mold-like staining measuring 18 by 24 inches plus two patches of 6 by 6 inches. What severity?","opts":["Below de minimis","Moderate","Severe — cumulative 3.5 square feet falls in the 1-to-9-square-foot band","Life-Threatening"],"ans":2,"exp":"432 plus 72 square inches equals 3.5 square feet cumulative in the room. Measure and add rather than eyeballing."},{"q":"Which statement about mold-like substance is correct?","opts":["A musty odor alone supports a citation","Odor is an indicator but not diagnostic; visual confirmation is required","Only laboratory-confirmed mold is citable","Color is irrelevant to identification"],"ans":1,"exp":"The standard is visual. Odor prompts a look; it does not by itself support a citation."},{"q":"A vacant unit shows mouse droppings and gnaw marks but no live rodents. Citable?","opts":["No — live pests are required","Yes — evidence such as droppings, shed skins, egg cases and gnaw marks is sufficient","Only if the resident reports it","Only in common areas"],"ans":1,"exp":"NSPIRE removed the live-sighting requirement. Advise clients to close entry points, not just clean, or they will be cited again."},{"q":"Pest evidence is found in four sampled units and the trash room. Severity?","opts":["Moderate — one per location","Low","Life-Threatening — infestation across multiple units and common areas escalates to the top tier on a 24-hour clock","Not citable without a pest control report"],"ans":2,"exp":"Extent across multiple units and common areas is the escalation trigger."},{"q":"Within a single unit, which observation indicates EXTENSIVE cockroach infestation?","opts":["Two live roaches in one location during the day","More than three live roaches observed simultaneously during the day, or at least one in more than one location","Any droppings at all","A single egg case"],"ans":1,"exp":"Three or fewer simultaneously in one location is evidence of cockroaches; more than three, or presence in more than one location, indicates extensive infestation."},{"q":"A CO alarm is missing in every unit of a gas-appliance building. How should you frame this for the client?","opts":["As a large score problem","As a small number of points because of the duplicate rule, but a very large liability because every unit needs an alarm on a 24-hour clock","As a deferred item under the affirmative requirements","As a Low deficiency"],"ans":1,"exp":"The duplicate rule limits the score effect to one Unit-area instance and one Inside-area instance; the correction obligation and the liability run to every unit."},{"q":"Which of these is currently CITED but NOT SCORED until October 1, 2026?","opts":["Blocked egress","Exposed conductor","A missing guardrail alongside an exterior stair","A gas leak"],"ans":2,"exp":"Guardrails, fire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI, HVAC, interior lighting and minimum electrical and lighting are the six deferred-scoring affirmative requirements. They are still cited and still must be corrected."},{"q":"What is the most common practical reason a client misses a 24-hour correction deadline?","opts":["They did not know the deficiency existed","The vendor could not be paid or the supervisor lacked spending authority","The repair was technically impossible","The inspector did not tell them"],"ans":1,"exp":"The failure is usually administrative, not technical — which is why the spending-authority letter and pre-approved vendor setup are the highest-value parts of the 24-hour protocol."}]},"ch6":{"title":"Chapter 6 Quiz — Severe & Moderate Deficiencies","questions":[{"q":"What is the exact relationship between the Severe and Moderate weights?","opts":["Severe is 1.5 times Moderate","Severe is exactly double Moderate in every area column","Severe is triple Moderate","The ratio varies by area"],"ans":1,"exp":"40.0 and 20.0 in the Unit column, 36.4 and 18.2 Inside, 33.1 and 16.5 Outside — exactly 2 to 1 throughout, which makes field triage easy."},{"q":"Roughly how many Low unit deficiencies equal one Moderate unit deficiency?","opts":["Two","Four","More than eight","Twenty-five"],"ans":2,"exp":"20.0 divided by 2.4 is about 8.3. Moderate and Low are an order of magnitude apart, not adjacent."},{"q":"A unit entry door closes and latches, but the deadbolt spins and will not throw. Severity?","opts":["Life-Threatening — it is a unit entry door","Severe — the resident's physical security is seriously compromised","Moderate","Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"A door that cannot CLOSE OR LATCH at all is the top-tier unit entry door finding. A door that closes but cannot be secured is Severe under the security language."},{"q":"A water heater's TPR discharge pipe terminates 11 inches above the floor drain. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — higher is safer","Deficiency — the discharge must terminate between 2 and 6 inches above the flood level","Compliant if the pipe is copper","Only a deficiency if the valve is also leaking"],"ans":1,"exp":"The 2-to-6-inch window is the criterion. Too high and too low are both defects, and this is missed constantly because people inspect the valve and not the pipe."},{"q":"Which of these is a listed TPR discharge piping deficiency?","opts":["Piping that slopes downward","Piping made of copper","Piping that is capped","Piping that terminates 3 inches above the drain pan"],"ans":2,"exp":"Damaged, capped, upward-sloping, or unsuitable-material piping are all listed deficiencies. A cap converts a relief valve into a pressure vessel."},{"q":"Paint thinner in its original container sits 18 inches from a fuel-burning water heater. Correct call?","opts":["Not a deficiency — it is in its original container","Deficiency — combustibles must not be stored within three feet of a heat appliance or fuel-burning water heater","Deficiency only if the container is open","Only a deficiency in common areas"],"ans":1,"exp":"Three feet is the criterion, and the original container does not exempt it. Correction cost is zero, which makes it a permanent punch-list item."},{"q":"Which is NOT excluded from the 18-inch sprinkler obstruction rule?","opts":["An exit sign 8 inches from a head","A wall-mounted kitchen cabinet","Boxes stacked to within 10 inches of a head","A parked vehicle 14 inches below a head"],"ans":2,"exp":"Permanent built-environment features and parked vehicles are excluded. Stored material is exactly what the rule targets."},{"q":"A bathroom has no fan, no passive vent, and a window painted shut. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — a window is present","Moderate deficiency — a painted-shut window is not operable, so none of the three accepted forms of ventilation is available","Severe","Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"The window must open easily and must not be sealed or painted over to count as one of the three accepted forms."},{"q":"A bathroom exhaust fan runs audibly but moves no air. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — it is present and running","Deficiency — present but not operable does not satisfy the requirement","Compliant if a window also exists but is painted shut","Advisory only"],"ans":1,"exp":"The tissue test demonstrates it. Present-but-nonfunctional is the same as absent for this standard."},{"q":"A bathroom has 2 square feet of mold-like substance AND a non-functioning exhaust fan. How many findings?","opts":["One — the mold subsumes the fan","Two separate findings under two separate standards: mold at Severe and ventilation at Moderate","One — the fan subsumes the mold","None if the mold is cleaned before the inspection"],"ans":1,"exp":"Two standards, two findings, combined Unit weight of 60.0. It is also the clearest root-cause-versus-symptom pairing in the program."},{"q":"A sidewalk has a 1/2-inch vertical lift. Correct call?","opts":["Trip hazard deficiency","Not a deficiency — the vertical criterion is 3/4 inch","Severe","Life-Threatening"],"ans":1,"exp":"Under 3/4 inch does not meet the vertical criterion. The horizontal criterion is a separation of at least 2 inches."},{"q":"At a sample of 20, what does one Low unit deficiency deduct?","opts":["2.4 points","1.2 points","0.24 points","0.12 points"],"ans":3,"exp":"2.4 divided by 20 equals 0.12. Low items do not decide outcomes and should never displace a Severe correction."},{"q":"When is chasing Low deficiencies a defensible strategy?","opts":["Always — every point counts","Never","When the fix is free, or when the property sits within a fraction of a point of an inspection-cycle boundary","Only at properties under 20 units"],"ans":2,"exp":"Two narrow, arithmetic-driven exceptions. Otherwise the money belongs higher on the severity ladder."},{"q":"A property provides a servicing company's invoice dated four months ago for a fire extinguisher whose tag is missing. Correct call?","opts":["Cite it — the tag is missing","Do not cite it — an invoice or report from the servicing company dated within the past year satisfies the requirement","Cite it at half weight","Cite it as Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"The exclusion is explicit. Knowing the exclusions keeps items off the punch list that were never going to be cited."},{"q":"How should you frame the six deferred-scoring affirmative requirements to a client?","opts":["They are optional until October 1, 2026","They are cited and must be corrected today, they do not affect the score before October 1, 2026, and the deduction lands in full on that date with no phase-in","They have been repealed","They only apply to new construction"],"ans":1,"exp":"Three sentences: live for compliance, deferred for scoring, no phase-in. Do not oversell the score risk — sell the value of planning a property-wide scope instead of discovering it inside a 30-day window."}]},"ch7":{"title":"Chapter 7 Quiz — HCV vs. Scored Programs","questions":[{"q":"Which programs receive a 0-100 NSPIRE score?","opts":["All HUD programs","Public Housing and project-based Multifamily (PBRA)","Housing Choice Voucher only","CPD programs only"],"ans":1,"exp":"Public Housing and PBRA are scored. HCV, PBV, Mod Rehab and the CPD programs are pass/fail on the individual unit."},{"q":"In the voucher and CPD programs, how are Low deficiencies handled?","opts":["They fail the unit","They are recorded but generally do not fail the unit and carry no required correction timeframe","They are not recorded at all","They must be corrected within 24 hours"],"ans":1,"exp":"Recorded, not failing, no required clock — which lets a small landlord aim limited money at what actually matters."},{"q":"What is the current compliance date for HCV, PBV and Section 8 Mod Rehab?","opts":["October 1, 2023","October 1, 2025","February 1, 2027","October 1, 2026"],"ans":2,"exp":"Extended three times, now February 1, 2027, carried in PIH Notice 2026-18 of July 15, 2026."},{"q":"What did PIH Notice 2026-18 say about the NSPIRE-V application?","opts":["It is now mandatory for all PHAs","It was decommissioned; HUD is no longer building an application for PHAs and paused development of its own software","It was renamed","It now includes a scoring calculator"],"ans":1,"exp":"PHAs are expected to use their own systems — which creates a real advisory opportunity ahead of February 2027."},{"q":"What is the CPD program compliance date?","opts":["October 1, 2023","October 1, 2026","February 1, 2027","January 1, 2028"],"ans":1,"exp":"ESG, CoC, HOME and HTF were extended to October 1, 2026."},{"q":"An HCV landlord misses a correction deadline. What happens to the subsidy?","opts":["It continues while repairs are scheduled","The PHA abates the housing assistance payment, and the abated amount is not retroactively restored","It is reduced by half","Nothing until the next annual inspection"],"ans":1,"exp":"Abated payments are lost permanently. That irrecoverable loss is the strongest argument for a pre-inspection service."},{"q":"Under the HOTMA-amended enforcement provisions at 24 CFR 982.404, what must the housing agency do if the unit still does not comply within 60 days of the noncompliance determination?","opts":["Issue a warning letter","Reduce the payment standard","Terminate the HAP contract","Re-inspect in 90 days"],"ans":2,"exp":"Termination is mandatory at that point, which is why the abatement window is a hard deadline rather than a negotiation."},{"q":"A voucher unit fails because the resident's furniture blocks a bedroom door. Whose problem is it?","opts":["The resident's — the PHA will address it with them","The owner's, even though the resident created the condition","Nobody's — resident property is exempt","The PHA's"],"ans":1,"exp":"Compliance runs to the owner. A pre-inspection resident letter plus a walk-through is the cheapest possible prevention."},{"q":"The annual self-inspection requirement covers:","opts":["A 10 percent sample of units","The same sample REAC would draw","All units, annually, with records retained three years and produced to HUD on request","Common areas only"],"ans":2,"exp":"All units, every year, records held three years. That combination is what makes it the best recurring product in the space."},{"q":"Why does a self-inspection surface information a REAC sample cannot?","opts":["It uses different standards","It covers every unit, so it reveals systemic conditions that repeat by floor plan or building","It is performed by the owner","It is scored differently"],"ans":1,"exp":"Systemic conditions are what actually decide REAC scores, and you can only see them reliably by walking everything."},{"q":"A score of 88 versus 90 changes what?","opts":["Nothing","The inspection interval — 80 to 89 is a two-year cycle, 90 and above is a three-year cycle","The passing threshold","The sample size"],"ans":1,"exp":"Over six years that is three inspection events instead of two, each with preparation cost, disruption and risk."},{"q":"How often are HCV units inspected?","opts":["Annually without exception","At least biennially, with a triennial option for qualifying small rural PHAs","Every three years","Only at initial lease-up"],"ans":1,"exp":"Biennial is the baseline, with a triennial option available to qualifying small rural PHAs."},{"q":"A 150-unit property has three bad units and one systemic missing-GFCI condition. Sample of 32. What should be fixed first?","opts":["The three bad units — they are the worst","The systemic condition — the sample may miss the three bad units but cannot miss a condition present everywhere","Neither; wait for the report","Whichever is cheapest"],"ans":1,"exp":"Bounded risk versus certainty. This reverses most managers' instincts, which run toward the visibly worst units."},{"q":"What is the correct way to describe sample preparation to a client?","opts":["Identify your best units and focus there","You cannot prepare the sample, you can only prepare the property","Request specific units from REAC","Prepare only ground-floor units"],"ans":1,"exp":"The sample is not knowable in advance, and the conditions that decide scores are present wherever it lands."},{"q":"When selling to a scored-program owner versus a voucher landlord, what changes?","opts":["Nothing — the standards are identical","The currency of the argument: points and inspection cycle for the scored owner, protected cash flow and avoided abatement for the landlord","Only the price","The severity definitions"],"ans":1,"exp":"Same standards, different consequence, therefore a different sale. Points for one, cash flow for the other."}]},"ch8":{"title":"Chapter 8 Quiz — Appeals & Building Your Business","questions":[{"q":"What is the NSPIRE-era name for the process of challenging an inspection result?","opts":["Database adjustment","Technical review","Score reconsideration","Administrative appeal"],"ans":1,"exp":"NSPIRE aligned the Public Housing and Multifamily provisions and retired the UPCS term 'database adjustment.' Using the old term dates you."},{"q":"Under the NSPIRE provisions for Multifamily, when must a technical review request be received by REAC?","opts":["Within 15 business days of the report","No later than the 45th calendar day following the day HUD provides the inspection report","Within 90 days of the final report","There is no deadline"],"ans":1,"exp":"Calendar days, not business days, running from report delivery. Calculate the date in writing at the top of the engagement letter."},{"q":"Which is NOT a recognized ground for technical review?","opts":["HUD or inspector error","Adverse conditions beyond the owner's control","The owner corrected the deficiency immediately","Conflict with state or local code"],"ans":2,"exp":"The four grounds are error, conditions beyond control, modernization in progress, and code conflict. Speed of repair is not among them."},{"q":"Mold-like substance was recorded above the 9-square-foot top-tier threshold. Your dated photograph with a tape measure in frame shows about 6 square feet. What is the argument?","opts":["Conditions beyond the owner's control","Misclassified severity under the inspector-error ground — a specific, checkable arithmetic argument","Modernization in progress","Code conflict"],"ans":1,"exp":"Measurement disputes are the strongest technical review arguments because they are objective and verifiable."},{"q":"Why does moving one Severe finding out of the Unit column matter disproportionately?","opts":["Unit findings cost double","Because Unit-area deductions are tested separately against the 30-point Unit Threshold, so the reclassification can be the difference between a 59 and a real score","Because Unit findings cannot be appealed","It does not matter"],"ans":1,"exp":"The Unit Threshold makes area attribution a pass/fail issue, not just a weighting issue."},{"q":"An elevator certificate is expired and the inspector recorded it as a deficiency. What is the argument?","opts":["Conditions beyond control","Criteria not met — under the standard the certificate is verified but neither its absence nor its expiration is itself a recorded deficiency","Modernization in progress","There is no argument"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is a clean criteria-not-met filing, and knowing it is the kind of detail that pays for a consultant."},{"q":"The single documentation habit that wins the most technical reviews is:","opts":["Writing longer narrative descriptions","Photographing every finding with a tape measure or ruler in the frame, timestamped","Collecting resident statements","Keeping copies of prior reports"],"ans":1,"exp":"Measured, dated photographic evidence converts a disagreement into arithmetic."},{"q":"A missing fire extinguisher tag is cited. What single document defeats it?","opts":["A photograph of the extinguisher","An invoice or report from the servicing company dated within the past year","A resident statement","A copy of the fire code"],"ans":1,"exp":"The standard provides that exception explicitly, which is why the certificate and service file is a product, not overhead."},{"q":"For an interim repair such as a blank cover plate over a missing receptacle, what must the record show?","opts":["Nothing further — the interim repair is the correction","That it is interim, plus the scheduled date of the full repair, which must occur within a reasonable timeframe approved by HUD or its designee","That the resident consented","That a permit was pulled"],"ans":1,"exp":"Interim measures can be acceptable where permitted, but they do not close the item; the full repair and its documentation do."},{"q":"Why should technical review support NOT be priced on pure contingency?","opts":["It is prohibited","It creates pressure to file weak grounds, and a pattern of weak filings damages your standing with the same field office you will face again","Contingency fees are untaxed","Clients will not pay them"],"ans":1,"exp":"Your credibility with the reviewing office is a long-term asset worth more than any single filing."},{"q":"In remediation management, what should your fee structure be designed to align with?","opts":["Volume of work ordered","Points recovered and money not wasted","Hours on site","Number of vendors engaged"],"ans":1,"exp":"A percentage of managed repair cost or a flat management fee aligns you with the client's outcome rather than with the size of the job."},{"q":"What is the strongest opening fact to lead with for a scored-program owner right now?","opts":["NSPIRE replaced UPCS in 2023","Six requirement categories begin deducting points on October 1, 2026, and are being cited today with no score effect","The passing score is 60","REAC uses a sample"],"ans":1,"exp":"Current, checkable, and consequential — it lands as expertise rather than as a pitch."},{"q":"What should the goal of a deliberately underpriced first engagement be?","opts":["Maximum revenue","A documented case study with real numbers — starting projection, items by severity, remediation cost, result","A long-term contract signed on day one","A referral fee"],"ans":1,"exp":"One documented case study with specific numbers opens more doors than any marketing material, and you cannot buy one."},{"q":"What is the first prerequisite to delegating field work?","opts":["Hiring an experienced inspector","A standardized field process — one checklist keyed to standard names, one photo protocol, one report template","A larger office","Certification"],"ans":1,"exp":"Until the process is standardized there is nothing to hand off, and quality will vary by whoever walked the property."},{"q":"What is the professionally correct response when you cannot verify a current standard?","opts":["Use your best recollection","Tell the client you are flagging it as unverified rather than guessing","Omit it from the report","Cite the prior year's version without noting it"],"ans":1,"exp":"The standards are versioned and revised. Flagging an unverified item is the only defensible practice, and clients remember it."}]}};
export const UNIT_TESTS = {"u1":{"title":"Unit 1 Cumulative Test — NSPIRE Foundations","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"NSPIRE replaced which two systems?","opts":["UPCS and REAC","UPCS and HQS","HQS and MOR","PASS and NSPIRE-V"],"ans":1,"exp":"NSPIRE merged UPCS (scored, sampled) and HQS (pass/fail, vouchers) into one standard."},{"q":"The three inspectable areas are:","opts":["Site, Building Exterior, Building Systems","Unit, Inside, Outside","Interior, Exterior, Systems","Dwelling, Common, Grounds"],"ans":1,"exp":"Unit, Inside and Outside — with Unit weighted highest at every severity."},{"q":"HUD defines a Severe deficiency as one presenting:","opts":["A high risk of death","A high risk of permanent disability, or serious injury or illness; or serious compromise of a resident's physical security or safety","A moderate risk of an adverse medical event","A condition critical to habitability with no substantive risk"],"ans":1,"exp":"The security clause is the half consultants most often forget, and it is why a broken deadbolt is Severe."},{"q":"Cumulative mold-like substance measuring 3 square inches in a room is:","opts":["Moderate","Low","Not recorded — below the 4-square-inch de minimis floor","Severe"],"ans":2,"exp":"Under 4 square inches cumulative in a room is below de minimis."},{"q":"Cumulative mold-like substance measuring 12 square feet in a room is:","opts":["Moderate","Severe","Life-Threatening","Not recorded"],"ans":2,"exp":"More than 9 square feet is the top tier with a 24-hour clock."},{"q":"A permanently installed window AC fills a bedroom's only egress window. Area and severity?","opts":["Outside, Moderate","Inside, Severe","Unit, Life-Threatening — blocked egress","Not citable"],"ans":2,"exp":"Blocked egress is always top tier, and the finding sits in the Unit area."},{"q":"An attached second-floor balcony belongs to which area?","opts":["Unit","Inside","Outside","It depends on whether it is enclosed"],"ans":2,"exp":"Attached balconies, porches, patios and carports are Outside components."},{"q":"Which six categories are cited but NOT scored until October 1, 2026?","opts":["Smoke alarms, CO alarms, egress, mold, infestation, sharp edges","Fire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI, guardrails, HVAC, interior lighting, minimum electrical and lighting","Elevators, boilers, sprinklers, fire alarms, chimneys, water heaters","Roofs, walkways, fencing, site lighting, playgrounds, signage"],"ans":1,"exp":"Joint Notice PIH 2025-27 / H 2025-05 postponed scoring of those six affirmative requirements."},{"q":"The current compliance date for HCV, PBV and Section 8 Mod Rehab is:","opts":["October 1, 2023","October 1, 2025","February 1, 2027","There is none"],"ans":2,"exp":"Extended three times; PIH Notice 2026-18 of July 15, 2026 carried the current guidance."},{"q":"Smoke and carbon monoxide alarm requirements were:","opts":["Postponed with the other affirmative requirements","Never part of any NSPIRE extension — they are statutory","Repealed in 2025","Made optional for Public Housing"],"ans":1,"exp":"Statutory in nature and in effect regardless of program or transition status."},{"q":"A minimum electrical and lighting requirement for a habitable room is:","opts":["One outlet","Two working outlets, or one working outlet and a permanently installed light fixture","Three outlets and a ceiling fan","A GFCI outlet"],"ans":1,"exp":"Plus at least one permanently installed light fixture in the kitchen and the bathroom."},{"q":"GFCI protection is required for outlets within:","opts":["3 feet of a water source","6 feet of a water source","10 feet of a water source","Any distance in a bathroom"],"ans":1,"exp":"Six feet, and protection may be provided at the receptacle or at the breaker."},{"q":"Correction timeframes in the scored Public Housing and Multifamily portfolios generally run:","opts":["All 24 hours","LT 24 hours, Severe 30 days, Moderate 30 days, Low 60 days","LT 48 hours, everything else 90 days","LT 24 hours, everything else 14 days"],"ans":1,"exp":"That is the general pattern, but the authoritative timeframe is printed on each standard, per program."},{"q":"The annual self-inspection requirement covers:","opts":["A 10 percent sample","The REAC sample","All units, annually, with records retained three years and produced on request","Common areas only"],"ans":2,"exp":"All units, every year, three-year retention — which makes it the best recurring product in the space."},{"q":"The best first question when a client describes a condition in adjectives is:","opts":["How much will it cost?","Which standard does it fall under, and which listed deficiency does it match?","How long has it been like that?","Has it been cited before?"],"ans":1,"exp":"Severity is a property of the written deficiency. Naming the standard produces the severity and the timeframe."}]},"u2":{"title":"Unit 2 Cumulative Test — Scoring & Deficiencies","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"State the scoring formula.","opts":["100 minus the number of deficiencies","100 minus the sum of (Defect Impact Weight x count) divided by the number of units in the inspected sample","100 minus deficiencies divided by total units at the property","The average of the three area scores"],"ans":1,"exp":"Weight, count, and the sample-size divisor."},{"q":"The Defect Impact Weight for a Life-Threatening deficiency in a unit is:","opts":["100.0","60.0","54.5","40.0"],"ans":1,"exp":"Unit 60.0, Inside 54.5, Outside 49.6."},{"q":"The Defect Impact Weight for a Moderate deficiency in the Outside area is:","opts":["20.0","18.2","16.5","2.0"],"ans":2,"exp":"Moderate: Unit 20.0, Inside 18.2, Outside 16.5."},{"q":"One Life-Threatening unit deficiency at a sample of 8 deducts:","opts":["60.0","7.50","3.00","1.88"],"ans":1,"exp":"60.0 divided by 8."},{"q":"Unit-area deductions total 32.0 and the raw score is 66. The result is:","opts":["Pass at 66","The Unit Threshold is triggered and the score is adjusted to 59","Pass at 60","Rescored without unit findings"],"ans":1,"exp":"Thirty or more points of Unit deduction caps the score at 59."},{"q":"The same deficiency under the same standard is found in six sampled units. For scoring:","opts":["Six deductions","One Unit-area deduction — though all six still require correction","Three deductions","Averaged across the sample"],"ans":1,"exp":"The duplicate rule collapses within an area, not across areas."},{"q":"A client fixes 19 of 20 instances of the same Severe unit deficiency. Score effect?","opts":["Nearly all the deduction is removed","None — the condition still exists in the Unit area","The severity drops","The deduction halves"],"ans":1,"exp":"Partial remediation of a repeat buys zero points, though it matters enormously for safety."},{"q":"The exposed-conductor criterion turns on an opening or gap greater than:","opts":["1/4 inch","1/2 inch","1 inch","2 inches"],"ans":1,"exp":"Carry a half-inch reference and measure the borderline cases."},{"q":"A working smoke alarm in a 1998 building uses a replaceable 9-volt battery. Compliant?","opts":["Yes — it works","No — alarms must be hardwired or have a sealed tamper-resistant 10-year battery","Yes if replaced annually","Only in Public Housing"],"ans":1,"exp":"The power source is part of the requirement, which is why this one surprises managers."},{"q":"A resident-installed deadbolt requires a key from the inside, kept on a hook. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — the key is accessible","Blocked egress — locks requiring a key, tool or special knowledge from the egress side are prohibited","Moderate","Not citable, resident-installed"],"ans":1,"exp":"Neither key placement nor resident installation is a defense."},{"q":"A water heater's TPR discharge terminates 11 inches above the floor drain. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant","Deficient — the compliant window is 2 to 6 inches above the flood level","Compliant if copper","Deficient only if the valve leaks"],"ans":1,"exp":"Too high and too low are both defects."},{"q":"Combustibles must not be stored within what distance of a heat appliance or fuel-burning water heater?","opts":["1 foot","3 feet","6 feet","10 feet"],"ans":1,"exp":"Three feet, and the original container does not exempt them."},{"q":"Which sprinkler condition IS a deficiency?","opts":["An exit sign 8 inches from a head","A wall cabinet 12 inches from a head","Boxes stacked to within 10 inches of a head","A parked vehicle 14 inches below a head"],"ans":2,"exp":"Permanent built-environment features and parked vehicles are excluded; stored material is the target."},{"q":"Pest evidence is found in four sampled units and the trash room. Severity?","opts":["Moderate","Low","Life-Threatening — extent across multiple units and common areas escalates it","Not citable without live pests"],"ans":2,"exp":"And evidence alone — droppings, shed skins, egg cases — supports citation without live insects."},{"q":"In the Unit column, how many Low deficiencies equal one Life-Threatening deficiency?","opts":["Five","Ten","Twenty-five","One hundred"],"ans":2,"exp":"60.0 divided by 2.4. Severity dominates location by more than an order of magnitude."}]},"u3":{"title":"Unit 3 Cumulative Test — Programs, Appeals & Business","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"Which programs receive a 0-100 NSPIRE score?","opts":["All HUD programs","Public Housing and project-based Multifamily","Housing Choice Voucher only","CPD programs only"],"ans":1,"exp":"The rest are pass/fail on the individual unit."},{"q":"An HCV landlord misses a correction deadline. What happens?","opts":["The subsidy continues while repairs are scheduled","The PHA abates the housing assistance payment, and the abated amount is not restored","The payment is reduced by half","Nothing until the next inspection"],"ans":1,"exp":"Irrecoverable loss is the strongest argument for a pre-inspection service."},{"q":"Under 24 CFR 982.404 as amended by HOTMA, what must the housing agency do if the unit is still non-compliant 60 days after the noncompliance determination?","opts":["Issue a warning","Re-inspect in 90 days","Terminate the HAP contract","Reduce the payment standard"],"ans":2,"exp":"Termination is mandatory at that point."},{"q":"Inspection frequency for scored properties runs:","opts":["Annually for all","90 and above every three years, 80 to 89 every two years, below 80 annually","Every two years for all","Set by the PHA"],"ans":1,"exp":"Which is why the gap between 88 and 90 is worth real money over a six-year window."},{"q":"The maximum number of units in a NSPIRE scoring sample is:","opts":["20","27","32","Uncapped"],"ans":2,"exp":"Raised from 27 under UPCS; the sample targets 90 percent confidence with a 6 percent margin of error."},{"q":"The NSPIRE-era name for challenging an inspection result is:","opts":["Database adjustment","Technical review","Score reconsideration","Administrative appeal"],"ans":1,"exp":"The UPCS term 'database adjustment' was retired."},{"q":"Under the NSPIRE provisions for Multifamily, a technical review request must be received by REAC:","opts":["Within 15 business days","No later than the 45th calendar day following the day HUD provides the report","Within 90 days of the final report","There is no deadline"],"ans":1,"exp":"Calendar days from report delivery. Calculate the date in writing immediately."},{"q":"Which is NOT a recognized ground for technical review?","opts":["HUD or inspector error","Adverse conditions beyond the owner's control","The owner corrected the deficiency immediately","Conflict with state or local code"],"ans":2,"exp":"Speed of repair is good practice, not a ground for restoring points."},{"q":"The single documentation habit that wins the most technical reviews is:","opts":["Longer narrative descriptions","Photographing every finding with a tape measure or ruler in frame, timestamped","Collecting resident statements","Keeping prior reports"],"ans":1,"exp":"Measured, dated evidence converts a disagreement into arithmetic."},{"q":"Why should technical review support NOT be priced on pure contingency?","opts":["It is prohibited","It creates pressure to file weak grounds, damaging your standing with the same field office you will face again","Clients will not pay it","It is untaxed"],"ans":1,"exp":"Credibility with the reviewing office is a long-term asset worth more than any single filing."}]}};

